{"@odata.context":"http://www.who.int/api/news/$metadata#diseaseoutbreaknews(Id,LastModified,PublicationDate,DateCreated,IncludeInSitemap,SystemSourceKey,UrlName,ItemDefaultUrl,Response,FurtherInformation,Summary,PublicationDateAndTime,TitleSuffix,UseOverrideTitle,Title,Epidemiology,OverrideTitle,Advice,Assessment,Overview,DonId,Provider)","value":[{"Id":"bfeabe5e-7ba7-40e4-aba0-ba714110cc21","LastModified":"2026-11-06T20:54:21Z","PublicationDate":"2026-11-06T17:27:28Z","DateCreated":"2026-11-06T17:27:28Z","IncludeInSitemap":true,"SystemSourceKey":null,"UrlName":"2026-DON625","ItemDefaultUrl":"/2026-DON625","Response":"<p>For detailed information about the ongoing public health response actions by the Ministry of Health, WHO and partners please refer to the latest situation reports published by the WHO Regional Office for Africa: <a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a>.. </p><p><strong>Democratic Republic of the Congo</strong></p><p>Health authorities in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in collaboration with WHO and partners, are continuing to implement and coordinate extensive public health measures. These include disease surveillance; laboratory testing; IPC; clinical care; community engagement; research; logistics; and support for response interventions and essential health services, including for the ongoing concurrent humanitarian crisis; engaging donors and mobilizing additional resources to address critical funding gaps and sustain response operations across affected and at-risk areas. A substantial scale-up is ongoing across all response pillars to get ahead of the outbreak. </p><p>On 23 October, the Tumaini platform vaccine trial was launched, with first participants enrolled in Bunia. Tumaini is a pivotal Phase 2/3 clinical trial of vaccine candidates against Bundibugyo virus (BDBV). The trial is an important step towards generating the evidence needed to better protect people against BVD and prevent future outbreaks. Based on available preclinical and early clinical evidence, <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/04-11-2026-two-new-candidate-vaccines-to-join-ebola-trial-as-treatment-research-advances\">expert groups convened by WHO have recommended adding two new BDBV vaccine candidates</a> to the ongoing Tumaini clinical trial in the Democratic Republic of the Congo for further clinical evaluation: ChAdOx1, developed by the University of Oxford with the Serum Institute of India, and Moderna&rsquo;s mRNA-1469. The vaccine candidates will enter the trial following national approvals. WHO and partners are actively supporting the trial with technical, scientific and operational expertise.</p><p><strong>Kenya</strong></p><p>Kenya has maintained a high level of Ebola preparedness since the outbreak began in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in May 2026; Screening continues at all designated points of entry and as of 5 November, a cumulative of 760 415 travellers have been screened. Health authorities in Kenya continue to implement public health measures including clinical care for the two remaining patients, including investigational therapeutics under approved protocols, testing at five designated laboratories including two mobile labs at the Ugandan border, and strengthening IPC measures. Psychosocial support is being provided to cases, contacts and health workers. Kenyan authorities continue to coordinate with counterparts in Uganda and the Democratic Republic of the Congo on cross-border surveillance. The 42-day countdown to the end of the outbreak will begin once the last patient is discharged. </p><p><strong>Nigeria</strong></p><p>The National and State incident management system has been activated by the Nigeria Centre for Disease Control and Prevention to coordinate response.&nbsp; WHO and partners are working closely with health authorities to strengthen control measures, including case investigation, contact tracing, infection prevention and control, and provision of essential supplies including personal protective equipment for health workers. Nigeria has maintained a high level of alert for BVD since the outbreak began in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in May 2026.&nbsp;</p>","FurtherInformation":"<ul></ul><p><strong>Regular Information products on the outbreak o</strong><strong>f Bundibugyo virus disease </strong><strong></strong></p><ul><li><strong>Daily update: </strong><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/alert-and-response\">Epidemiological update on BVD outbreak in Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li><li><strong>Published weekly: </strong><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a> | WHO| Regional Office for Africa </li><li><strong>Published biweekly:</strong><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news\"><strong></strong>Disease Outbreak News | All Hazards Public Health Events, Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li></ul><p><strong>Additional information</strong></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Current outbreak: declarations and status</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.admin.ch/en/newnsb/hKpTLMT8HgXi\">Official press release of Switzerland on the medical evacuation</a></li><li>Official press release on <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/news/uk-citizen-returned-from-drc-for-specialist-ebola-care\">UK citizen returned from DRC for specialist Ebola care</a></li><li>Official public notification of the Federal Government of Nigeria: <a href=\"https://health.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Immediate-Press-statememnt_confirmed-Ebola-in-Nigeria-1.pdf\">Immediate-Press-statememnt_confirmed-Ebola-in-Nigeria-1.pdf</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/nigeria/news/nigeria-confirms-imported-case-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease\">Nigeria confirms imported case of Ebola Bundibugyo virus disease | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a></li><li>Official public notification of the Ministry of Health of Kenya: <a href=\"https://x.com/MOH_Kenya/status/2107407663231558024\">Ministry of Health (@MOH_Kenya) on X</a></li><li>Kenya D<a href=\"https://x.com/NPHIKenya/status/2118237899950793105?s=46\">irectorate of Public Health Intelligence &amp; Population Health: Daily PoE BVD Screening Report</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/05-06-2026-africa-cdc-and-who-launch-joint-continental-ebola-response-plan\">Africa CDC and WHO launch joint continental Ebola response plan</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/publications/bundibugyo-ebola-virus-continental-preparedness-and-response-plan-june-november-2026\">Bundibugyo Ebola virus | Continental preparedness and response plan: June-November 2026</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern\">Epidemic of Ebola Disease caused by Bundibugyo virus in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda determined a public health emergency of international concern</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://administration.sante.gouv.cd/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Declaration-de-la-17e-Epidemie-de-la-maladie-a-virus-Ebola-dans-les-zones-de-sante-de-Rwampara-Mongwalu-et-Bunia-dans-la-province-dIturi.pdf\">The Ministry of Public Health, Hygiene and Social Welfare, DRC, officially declares the 17th Ebola Disease outbreak</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/democratic-republic-congo-confirms-new-ebola-outbreak-who-scales-upsupport\">WHO Democratic Republic of Congo confirms new Ebola outbreak</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/28-05-2026-message-by-the-who-director-general-to-the-people-of-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo\">Message by the WHO Director-General to the people of the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/frontline-trust-day-julienne-anoko-whos-ebola-community-engagement-officer-drc\">At the frontline of trust: a day with Julienne Anoko WHO's Ebola Community Engagement Officer in DRC</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/decentralized-testing-speeds-ebola-response-democratic-republic-congo\">Decentralized testing speeds up Ebola response in the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/uganda/news/uganda-ends-ebola-outbreak-following-completion-42-day-countdown\">Uganda ends Ebola outbreak following completion of 42-day countdown | WHO | Regional Office for Afri&hellip;</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\">Second meeting of the IHR Emergency Committee on the epidemic of Ebola Bundibugyo virus disease in the Democratic Republic of the Congo &ndash; Temporary recommendations</a></li></ul><p><strong><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></strong><strong>WHO Rapid risk assessments</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment-ebola-disease-caused-by-bundibugyo-virus--democratic-republic-of-the-congo-v4\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment-Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo v4</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span><p><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span><strong>Epidemiological updates and situation reports</strong></p></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Weekly External Situation Report. EBOLA BUNDIBUGYO VIRUS DISEASE OUTBREAK Democratic Republic of the Congo </a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/ebola/situation-summary/index.html\">Ebola Outbreak: Current Situation | Ebola | CDC</a></li><li><a href=\"https://insp.cd/category/sitrep/\">Daily situation report, Ministry of Health, Democratic Republic of the Congo.</a></li></ul><p><strong>Published Disease Outbreak News (current outbreak)</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON614\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 1 August 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON615\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 14 August 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON616\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 28 August 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON617\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 11 September 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/india/home/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON618\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 25 September 2026</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON619\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 8 October 2026</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON622\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 23 October 2026</a></li></ul><p><strong>Clinical management, IPC, and occupational safety</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/B09774\">WHO guidelines for the clinical management of filovirus disease</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240111332\">Infection prevention and control guideline for Ebola and Marburg disease. WHO.17 May 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240107205\">Infection prevention and control and water, sanitation and hygiene in health facilities during Ebola or Marburg disease outbreaks: rapid assessment tool, user guide.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240107328\">Assessment and management of health and care workers with possible occupational exposures to Orthoebolavirus or Orthomarburgvirus: implementation guidance</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241515894\">Optimized Supportive Care for Ebola Virus Disease. Clinical management standard operating procedures. WHO. 2019</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240032729\">Framework and toolkit for infection prevention and control in outbreak preparedness, readiness and response at the national level</a></li><li><a href=\"https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/386711\">Diagnostic testing for Ebola disease and Marburg virus disease: interim guidance, 9 July 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/considerations-for-border-health-and-points-of-entry-for-filovirus-disease-outbreaks\">Considerations for border health and points of entry for filovirus disease outbreaks</a></li></ul><p><strong>&nbsp;</strong><strong>Vaccines</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/04-11-2026-two-new-candidate-vaccines-to-join-ebola-trial-as-treatment-research-advances\">Two new candidate vaccines to join Ebola trial as treatment research advances</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/B09865\">Third meeting of the WHO Technical Advisory Group on candidate vaccine prioritization (TAG-CVP) for Bundibugyo virus disease outbreak response: meeting report, 31 July 2026</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\">&nbsp; </span></li><li>WHO press release regarding a clinical trial to find effective treatments against Bundibugyo virus disease.</li></ul><p><strong>&nbsp;</strong><strong>Training</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/26-03-2025-who-launches-online-training-to-strengthen-filovirus-outbreak-response\">WHO Launches Online Training to Strengthen Filovirus Outbreak Response</a><p><strong></strong><strong>Prior Bundibugyo virus disease events, DRC (2012)</strong></p></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2012_09_14-en\">Disease Outbreak News. Ebola outbreak in Democratic Republic of Congo &ndash; update. WHO. 14 September 2012</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2012_10_26-en\">Disease Outbreak News. Ebola outbreak in Democratic Republic of Congo &ndash; update. WHO. 26 October 2012</a></li></ul><p><strong>&nbsp;</strong><strong>Background and reference</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ebola-disease\">Ebola disease fact sheet</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/ebola-vaccines\">Ebola vaccines</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Citable reference:</strong> World Health Organization (6 November 2026). Disease Outbreak News; Bundibugyo Virus Disease, Democratic Republic of the Congo. Available at:\u202f<a href=\"https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON625\">https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON625</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p>","Summary":"Since the publication of the previous Disease Outbreak News on 23 October 2026, the Bundibugyo virus disease (BVD) outbreak has expanded geographically in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. \r\n\r\nWhile some areas have seen a slight decline in cases, sustained, high-level transmission continues overall in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. As of 3 November 2026, a total of 10 668 confirmed cases, including 5198 deaths (crude case fatality ratio: 48.7%), have been reported across 67 health zones in eight provinces, with active transmission ongoing in 51 health zones.  The high proportion of deaths occurring in communities, especially among young children, continues to highlight challenges in early case detection and timely access to appropriate care. Of the 512 deaths reported since 21 October, 335 occurred in communities and 177 in treatment centres.\r\n\r\nSince the last Disease Outbreak News, no new cases have been reported from Kenya or Nigeria. However, the high-level transmission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo indicates a continued need for readiness in the region, including cross-border surveillance, points of entry monitoring, timely case detection and coordinated preparedness and response measures. No new country has reported cases since the last Disease Outbreak News was published, however, two countries have medically evacuated confirmed cases among citizens: Switzerland and the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland.\r\n\r\nBased on the currently available information, WHO advises against any restriction of travel to, or trade with, States Parties experiencing and/or responding to the public health event. \r\n","PublicationDateAndTime":"2026-11-06T20:42:12Z","TitleSuffix":"","UseOverrideTitle":true,"Title":"Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Kenya and Nigeria","Epidemiology":"<p>Bundibugyo virus disease (BVD) is a severe Ebola disease caused by the Bundibugyo virus, one of the <em>Orthoebolavirus </em>species. It is a zoonotic disease, with fruit bats suspected to be the natural reservoir.</p><p>Human infection is thought to initially occur through close contact with the blood or secretions of infected wildlife, such as bats or non-human primates, and it subsequently spreads from person-to-person through direct contact with the blood, secretions, organs, or other bodily fluids of infected individuals or contaminated surfaces and materials. Transmission is particularly amplified in health-care settings when infection prevention and control (IPC) measures are inadequate, and during unsafe burial practices involving direct contact with deceased individuals.</p><p>The incubation period for BVD ranges from two to 21 days, and infected individuals are not infectious until symptom onset. Early symptoms such as fever, fatigue, muscle pain, headache, and sore throat are non-specific, which complicates clinical diagnosis and can delay detection. These symptoms then progress to gastrointestinal symptoms, organ dysfunction, and, in some cases, haemorrhagic manifestations.</p><p>The CFR from the previous two BVD outbreaks, reported in Uganda and in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in 2007 and 2012, were 30% and 50%, respectively.</p><p>Differentiating BVD from other endemic febrile illnesses such as malaria is challenging without laboratory confirmation. Outbreak control relies on rapid case identification and investigation, isolation and care, contact tracing, safe burials and strong community engagement, as no approved vaccines or specific treatments currently exist for BVD.</p><p>Since first detected in May 2026, this BVD outbreak has rapidly evolved into a large and geographically expanding epidemic in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, with sustained transmission, high mortality and an increasing risk of further international spread. The current outbreak is the second documented Bundibugyo virus disease outbreak in the country, after the 2012 outbreak, and the largest Ebola disease outbreak ever recorded in the Democratic Republic of the Congo irrespective of virus species. The population at greatest risk of exposure is concentrated in communities living in and moving through the health areas with active transmission.&nbsp;</p>","OverrideTitle":"Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo, Kenya and Nigeria","Advice":"<p>Based on the currently available information, WHO advises against any restriction of travel to, or trade with, States Parties experiencing and/or responding to the public health event. The updated <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\">Temporary Recommendations</a> issued to States Parties on 24 August 2026 underscore the importance of coordinated outbreak control, strengthened cross\u2011border collaboration, and sustained surveillance and preparedness to prevent further regional spread and ensure an effective public health response. Rapid recognition of cases, testing and optimized supportive care can reduce mortality, and improve community perceptions and acceptance of health care within the response.</p>","Assessment":"<p>On 14 August 2026, WHO reassessed the risk of the outbreak of BVD. The risk for countries sharing land borders with the Democratic Republic of the Congo was separated from the risk for other countries in the African Region. </p><p>The risk in the Democratic Republic of the Congo was assessed as very high, the risk for countries sharing land borders with the Democratic Republic of the Congo was assessed as high, and the risks for the rest of the African region and at the global level was again assessed as low. For further information, please see <a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment-ebola-disease-caused-by-bundibugyo-virus--democratic-republic-of-the-congo-v4\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment-Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo v4</a>.</p><p>In light of the evolving situation and newly available information, the rapid risk assessment is currently being revised to reassess the risk in affected countries, the African region and globally. &nbsp;</p>","Overview":"<p><p>Cases have been reported in five countries since the declaration of the outbreak on 15 May 2026: the Democratic Republic of the Congo, France, Kenya, Nigeria, and Uganda. The outbreaks in France and Uganda have been controlled since August while Kenya and Nigeria continue with ongoing response. &nbsp;</p><p><strong>Democratic Republic of the Congo</strong></p><p>An additional 964 confirmed cases, including 511 confirmed deaths, have been reported in the Democratic Republic of the Congo since the previous <a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON622\">Disease Outbreak News</a> was published. </p><p>On 5 November 2026, <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/news/uk-citizen-returned-from-drc-for-specialist-ebola-care\">the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland reported the medical evacuation of one confirmed case</a> (a health and care worker) from the Democratic Republic of the Congo; the case is now receiving medical care in London.</p><p>In the last 21-day period, as of 3 November, 1463 new confirmed cases were reported, with Ituri accounting for 710 cases, Nord Kivu for 640 cases, Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; for 77 cases, Tshopo for 31 cases and Nord-Ubangi for 5 cases. No new cases were reported in Bas-U&eacute;l&eacute;, Sud Kivu or Sud-Ubangi during that period. </p><p>As of 3 November, cumulative confirmed cases have reached 10 668, including 5198 deaths and 2881 recoveries. The overall crude CFR is 48.7%. </p><p>Confirmed cases have now been identified in 67 health zones across eight provinces. Fifty-one of the 67 affected health zones reported at least one new case during the preceding 21 days, while 16 reported no recent cases. Beni, Bunia, and Mandima have reported the highest number of cases and deaths in the preceding 21 days. Ituri remains the most extensively affected province, with 28 of 36 health zones reporting cases during this outbreak, followed by Nord Kivu (17/34), Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; (7/13), Tshopo (7/23), Bas-U&eacute;l&eacute; (4/11), Nord-Ubangi (2/11), Sud Kivu (1/34) and Sud-Ubangi (1/16). Yakoma in Nord-Ubangi province is the most recently affected health zone, reporting three confirmed cases.<br /></p><p><strong>Figure 1. Classification of health zones reporting confirmed cases of Bundibugyo virus disease in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, as of 3 November 2026</strong></p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/figure_1_bvd_health_zone_classification_map.png?sfvrsn=c31e1fa_2\" height=\"1190\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"Figure_1_BVD_health_zone_classification_map\" width=\"1540\" alt=\"Figure 1 6.11.2026\" sf-size=\"267083\" /><br /></p><p>While Nord Kivu reported the highest number of cases in the last seven days (232 cases as of 3 November), Ituri remains the most affected province, with 7485 cumulative confirmed cases and 3532 deaths. Nord Kivu follows with 2593 confirmed cases and 1422 deaths, and continues to record the highest provincial CFR, at 54.8%. Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; has reported 470 cases and 208 deaths, while Tshopo has recorded 94 cases and 29 deaths. The less-affected provinces continue to report substantially fewer cases, but transmission persists in several of them. The continued occurrence of cases across multiple provinces shows that the outbreak remains geographically active even as intensity varies between health zones.</p><p>As of 3 November 2026, the average proportion of contacts seen daily over the previous 14-day period is 86%, remaining below the required threshold of 95.0%. Three provinces were at or above the 95.0% threshold for a daily average of the proportion of contacts seen over the previous 14-day period: Bas-Uele (99%, 435/440), Nord-Ubangi (95%, 28/29) and Sud-Ubangi (95%; 62/65). Four provinces were below the threshold: Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; (85%; 1207/1420), Ituri (91% (9589/10 491), Tshopo (71%; 489/685) and Nord-Kivu (82%; 11 190/13 716). Strengthened contact follow-up is required, particularly in provinces below the target, to achieve the minimum threshold of 95.0%.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Figure 2: Number of confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, by date of notification, as of 3 November 2026</strong></p><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/figure_2_bvd_confirmed_cases_epidemic_curve.png?sfvrsn=3239f036_2\" height=\"998\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"Figure_2_BVD_confirmed_cases_epidemic_curve\" width=\"1300\" alt=\"Figure 2 6 November 2026\" sf-size=\"69814\" /></p><p><strong>Figure 3: Number of deaths among confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in the Democratic Republic of the Congo by date of notification, as of 3 November 2026&nbsp;</strong></p><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/figure_3_bvd_confirmed_deaths_epidemic_curve.png?sfvrsn=bbea1523_2\" height=\"998\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"Figure_3_BVD_confirmed_deaths_epidemic_curve\" width=\"1300\" alt=\"Figure 3. 6 November 2026\" sf-size=\"64043\" /></p><p><strong>Kenya </strong></p><p>As of 3 November 2026, the cumulative confirmed cases reported in Kenya remains at nine. Since the last Disease Outbreak News, there has been one additional death, resulting in a total of two deaths reported in Kenya and a crude CFR of 22.2%. &nbsp;All eight secondary cases are epidemiologically linked to the imported index case and associated with one cluster (four family members of the index case and four health and care workers). &nbsp;As of 3 November, of the nine cases reported. five have recovered (all five were discharged on 3 November after meeting discharge criteria) while two cases are still receiving treatment.&nbsp; </p><p>There is currently no evidence of transmission outside this cluster and there have been no new confirmed cases since 19 October 2026.&nbsp; Additionally, all eight secondary cases were already under quarantine as known contacts when they developed symptoms. &nbsp;</p><p>As of 3 November, of the 135 cumulative contacts identified since the first confirmed case, eight subsequently became confirmed cases and nine contacts left Kenya (relevant National IHR Focal Points were notified). Of the remaining 118 contacts, 66 have completed the 21-day follow-up and 52 remain under monitoring, with follow-up expected to end on 11 November 2026. </p><p><strong>Figure 4: Number of confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in Kenya, by date of notification, as of 3 November 2026</strong></p><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/figure-4.png?sfvrsn=7c5d0826_2\" height=\"1265\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"Figure 4\" width=\"2317\" alt=\"Figure 4 6 November 2026\" sf-size=\"62728\" /></p><p><strong>Nigeria</strong></p><p>On 20 October 2026, Nigeria reported one laboratory-confirmed imported case of BVD in the Federal Capital Territory Abuja. On 26 October 2026, <a href=\"https://www.admin.ch/en/newnsb/hKpTLMT8HgXi\">Switzerland reported the medical evacuation of the case</a>, who remains in isolation and receiving medical care in Geneva, Switzerland. </p><p>As of 3 November, a cumulative of 62 contacts had been identified in Nigeria for follow up. Six contacts have successfully completed the 21-day follow-up period. National authorities, with support from WHO and partners, have intensified contact tracing, case investigation and infection prevention and control measures, including the provision of essential supplies and personal protective equipment for health workers. Investigations are ongoing to establish the likely source of infection and reconstruct the travel and exposure history. WHO has urged countries across the region to strengthen surveillance, laboratory diagnostic capacity, rapid response readiness and cross-border preparedness to mitigate the risk of further transmission.</p>","DonId":"2026-DON625","Provider":"dynamicProvider372"},{"Id":"ed87efed-9b8e-492e-ad7d-1e1e22e9f77b","LastModified":"2026-11-06T14:45:20Z","PublicationDate":"2026-11-06T09:01:14Z","DateCreated":"2026-11-06T09:01:14Z","IncludeInSitemap":true,"SystemSourceKey":null,"UrlName":"2026-DON624","ItemDefaultUrl":"/2026-DON624","Response":"<p>National authorities, WHO and partners are responding to yellow fever activity across affected and at-risk countries in the African Region. Response activities include enhanced surveillance and case investigation, laboratory testing and specimen referral, active case finding, risk communication and community engagement, and the planning or implementation of vaccination activities.</p><p>WHO is:</p><ul><li>closely monitoring yellow fever epidemiology, transmission patterns and risk trends and supporting strengthened surveillance, case investigation, laboratory diagnosis and specimen referral;</li><li>supporting affected and at-risk Member States to assess population immunity and plan and implement appropriate preventive and reactive vaccination activities;</li><li>providing technical support for clinical case management, infection prevention and control and other key outbreak-response capacities;</li><li>supporting risk communication and community engagement, including community-based surveillance where appropriate;</li><li>supporting cross-border preparedness, information sharing and coordination among affected and at-risk countries and partners;</li><li>supporting operational readiness, including access to vaccines and other essential commodities required for preparedness and response; and</li><li>continuing to work with partners to strengthen implementation of the Eliminate Yellow Fever Epidemics (EYE) Strategy and support coordinated preparedness and response activities.</li></ul>","FurtherInformation":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment--yellow-fever--who-african-region-v.1\" target=\"_blank\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment: Yellow fever, WHO African Region, version 1, published 30 October 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON610\">WHO Disease Outbreak News 610: Yellow fever &ndash; Global, published 24 June 2026</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/yellow-fever\">WHO Yellow fever fact sheet</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/initiatives/eye-strategy\">WHO Eliminate Yellow Fever Epidemics (EYE) Strategy 2017&ndash;2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/outbreak-toolkit/disease-outbreak-toolboxes/yellow-fever-outbreak-toolbox\" target=\"_blank\">WHO Yellow fever outbreak toolbox</a></li><li><a href=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/documents/emergencies/travel-advice/yellow-fever-vaccination-requirements-country-list-2020-en264bd6ca-d536-4835-93be-ef83c10f4b00.pdf?sfvrsn=3430b1a2_1\">WHO recommendations on country vaccination requirements and recommendations for international travellers - WHO country list for yellow fever vaccination requirements and recommendations</a></li><li>International Coordinating Group on Vaccine Provision: yellow fever - <a href=\"https://www.who.int/groups/icg/yellow-fever\" target=\"_blank\">ICG on Vaccine Provision: Yellow fever</a></li></ul><p><strong>Citable reference:</strong> World Health Organization (6 November 2026). Disease Outbreak News; Yellow fever in WHO African Region. Available at:&nbsp; <a href=\"https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON624\">https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON624 </a>&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</p>","Summary":"Yellow fever transmission continues in the WHO African Region, with recent outbreak activity concentrated in parts of West Africa. Between 1 January and 12 October 2026, 67 confirmed cases, including 18 deaths, were recorded in nine countries in the Region: Burkina Faso, Cameroon, the Central African Republic, C\u00f4te d\u2019Ivoire, Ghana, Guinea, Liberia, Nigeria and Togo. The most recent outbreak activity has been concentrated in Burkina Faso, C\u00f4te d\u2019Ivoire, Ghana and Liberia, including in areas close to international borders. \r\n\r\nOccupational and environmental exposures, including work in or near forested areas, mining activities, and other outdoor occupations, are important risk factors for yellow fever infection. Frequent cross-border population movement, the presence of competent mosquito vectors, and uneven vaccination coverage increase the risk of further transmission and geographic spread.\r\n\r\nCountries in the Region, supported by WHO and partners, are implementing response activities including enhanced surveillance and case investigation, laboratory testing, active case finding, risk communication and community engagement, and vaccination activities in affected and at-risk areas. \r\n\r\nOn 27 October 2026, WHO assessed the overall public health risk associated with the current yellow fever situation as moderate at the regional level. The global risk remains low.","PublicationDateAndTime":"2026-11-06T19:00:00Z","TitleSuffix":"","UseOverrideTitle":true,"Title":"Yellow fever - WHO African Region","Epidemiology":"<p>Yellow fever is an acute viral haemorrhagic disease transmitted by infected mosquitoes. In the African Region, yellow fever circulates between mosquitoes and non-human primates in forested areas and can also spread to and between humans through infected mosquitoes in urban environments. The incubation period is generally three to six days.</p><p>Infection may be asymptomatic or initially present with fever, headache, body aches, nausea, vomiting and weakness. Approximately 15% of infected people develop severe disease, which may include recurrent high fever, jaundice, bleeding, shock and organ failure. Approximately half of patients who enter this severe phase die within seven to 10 days.</p><p>Diagnosis can be challenging because early symptoms are non-specific and may resemble malaria, leptospirosis, viral hepatitis, dengue and other haemorrhagic fevers. Laboratory confirmation relies on molecular testing during the early stage of infection and serological testing, including enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) or PRNT, during later stages.</p><p>There is no specific antiviral treatment routinely recommended for yellow fever and clinical management is primarily supportive. Vaccination is the most effective preventive measure, with a single dose providing lifelong protection.</p>","OverrideTitle":"Yellow fever - WHO African Region","Advice":"<p>Vaccination is the most effective measure for preventing yellow fever. A single dose of yellow fever vaccine provides lifelong protection. Countries at risk should maintain high population immunity through routine immunization and appropriate preventive and outbreak-response vaccination activities, guided by epidemiological risk and identified immunity gaps.<br /></p> <p><strong>Surveillance</strong><strong></strong></p><p>WHO encourages Member States with areas at risk for yellow fever to maintain strong epidemiological surveillance to enable early detection and timely response. Surveillance should include systematic investigation and laboratory testing of suspected cases, active case finding in affected and neighbouring areas, and assessment of probable sites of infection and vaccination status. Cross-border coordination and timely information sharing are particularly important where cases occur close to international borders.<br /></p> <p><strong>Laboratory diagnosis</strong><strong></strong></p><p>Rapid collection and referral of appropriate specimens and timely laboratory confirmation are important for directing public health action. Molecular methods are particularly useful during the early phase of infection, while serological tests can support diagnosis later in the course of illness. Serological findings should be interpreted in the context of previous yellow fever vaccination and possible cross-reactivity with other flaviviruses.<br /></p> <p><strong>Vaccination</strong><strong></strong></p><p>WHO recommends yellow fever vaccination as the principal means of preventing infection. Vaccination activities should prioritize populations at risk and areas with identified immunity gaps. Preventive and reactive campaigns should be guided by updated epidemiological and risk assessments, with particular attention to populations living in or travelling to affected and high-risk areas.<br /></p> <p><strong>Vector control and risk communication</strong><strong></strong></p><p>Integrated mosquito surveillance and control, reduction of mosquito breeding sites and personal measures to prevent mosquito bites complement vaccination. Risk communication and community engagement should promote awareness of yellow fever symptoms, vaccination and timely care-seeking among populations at risk.<br /></p> <p><strong>International travel and trade</strong><strong></strong></p><p>Countries affected by yellow fever outbreaks should reinforce implementation of the <em>International Health Regulations (IHR 2005)</em> at points of entry and along major land-border crossings to mitigate the risk of international spread and facilitate coordinated cross-border response activities.</p><p>All international travellers aged nine months and older travelling to areas at risk of yellow fever transmission, as defined by WHO, are advised to be vaccinated. Under the International Health Regulations (2005), countries may require proof of yellow fever vaccination from incoming and/or outgoing travellers. One documented dose of a WHO-approved yellow fever vaccine confers lifelong protection and is accepted as valid for the purposes of the International Certificate of Vaccination or Prophylaxis.</p><p>Based on the currently available information, WHO advises against any restriction of travel to, or trade with, States Parties experiencing / responding to the public health event.</p>","Assessment":"<p>On 27 October 2026, <a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment--yellow-fever--who-african-region-v.1\">WHO assessed the overall risk associated with the current yellow fever</a> situation as moderate in the WHO African Region, due to the likelihood of continued transmission, the potential for geographical and cross-border spread, existing immunity gaps and operational constraints affecting outbreak control. The global risk remains low.<br /></p> <p>Continued transmission and additional human cases in the African Region are considered likely. &nbsp;Several affected districts have previously identified immunity gaps, including areas where preventive vaccination has not been implemented or where population immunity remains insufficient despite previous vaccination activities. Unvaccinated populations are at highest risk, particularly rural and forest-edge communities, mobile and cross-border populations and people who work in forested areas, agriculture or mining.<br /></p> <p>The occurrence of yellow fever activity in areas close to international borders increases the potential for further geographical and cross-border spread. Frequent cross-border travel and movement linked to trade, farming, livestock and mining could contribute to spread between affected and neighbouring countries. Introduction of the virus into densely populated urban or peri-urban areas where the <em>Aedes aegypti</em> mosquito is established and population immunity is low could result in larger outbreaks and place additional pressure on response capacities.<br /></p> <p>The Region has important capacities for yellow fever prevention and response, including established surveillance systems, a regional laboratory network, routine and reactive vaccination mechanisms, outbreak-response experience, access to the international emergency vaccine stockpile and established response and cross-border coordination mechanisms. However, persistent immunity gaps, surveillance and laboratory challenges, delays in specimen referral and confirmation, insecurity, access constraints, limited resources and competing public health emergencies can affect timely detection, investigation and control.</p>","Overview":"<p>Yellow fever transmission continued across the WHO African Region during 2026, with recent outbreak activity concentrated in parts of West Africa, including areas close to international borders.<br /></p><p>Between 1 January and 12 October 2026, 67 confirmed cases, including 18 deaths, were recorded in nine countries. The most recent outbreak activity was concentrated in Burkina Faso, C&ocirc;te d&rsquo;Ivoire, Ghana and Liberia.<br /></p><p>In Burkina Faso, 15 confirmed cases, including three deaths, had been reported as of the week ending 23 August 2026, corresponding to a case fatality ratio of 20%.<br /></p><p>In C&ocirc;te d&rsquo;Ivoire, 30 confirmed cases, including 10 deaths, had been reported as of 23 September 2026, corresponding to a case fatality ratio of 33%. Seven cases were confirmed by plaque reduction neutralization testing (PRNT) and 23 by polymerase chain reaction (PCR). Nine districts across seven regions were affected, with Bounkani Region accounting for 18 of the 30 confirmed cases.<br /></p><p>In Ghana, four cases, confirmed by PCR, including three deaths had been reported as of 2 September 2026.<br /></p><p>In Liberia, one laboratory-confirmed case had been reported as of 23 September 2026.<br /></p><p>Confirmed cases were also reported in Cameroon (nine cases, including one death), Nigeria (four cases, including one death), the Central African Republic (two cases), Guinea (one case) and Togo (one case). No deaths among confirmed cases were reported from the Central African Republic, Guinea or Togo.<br /></p><p>Country-specific figures reflect the latest information available to WHO as of 12 October 2026; reporting cut-off dates differ by country.</p><p>The detection of yellow fever in these countries indicates increased transmission across parts of West Africa. Although epidemiological links between the events have not been established, several affected areas are close to international borders and have substantial population mobility and occupational and environmental exposure in or near forested areas. Together with heterogeneous population immunity and favourable ecological conditions for mosquito vectors, these factors sustain the risk of further spread within affected countries and across national borders.</p><p><strong>Confirmed\r\nyellow fever cases and recently affected areas in the WHO African Region, 2026&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</strong><strong style=\"background-color:transparent;font-family:'Noto Sans', Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\"></strong><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">Data\r\nas of 12 October 2026</span></p><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/afro-yellow-fever-cases-2026-don.png?sfvrsn=5806aa2f_2\" height=\"4961\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"AFRO Yellow Fever cases 2026 DON\" width=\"4961\" alt=\"Yellow fever AFRO October 2026\" sf-size=\"952384\" /></p><p></p>","DonId":"2026-DON624","Provider":"dynamicProvider372"},{"Id":"69b96fe8-8b6d-48f4-b59b-204c444d66c5","LastModified":"2026-11-03T08:48:28Z","PublicationDate":"2026-11-02T14:26:51Z","DateCreated":"2026-11-02T14:26:51Z","IncludeInSitemap":true,"SystemSourceKey":null,"UrlName":"2026-DON623","ItemDefaultUrl":"/2026-DON623","Response":"<ul><li>Notification by authorities to WHO and regional bodies.&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.santepubliquefrance.fr/les-actualites/virus-shamonda-un-premier-cas-humain-detecte-en-france-que-sait\">Clinical, biological and epidemiological investigations</a> are continuing in conjunction with experts in human, animal and environmental health.</li></ul>","FurtherInformation":"<div>&bull;\tMinistry of Health, Families, Autonomy and Disability Affairs, France: <a href=\"https://sante.gouv.fr/actualites-presse/presse/communiques-de-presse/article/virus-shamonda-une-premiere-detection-chez-l-etre-humain\">https://sante.gouv.fr/actualites-presse/presse/communiques-de-presse/article/virus-shamonda-une-premiere-detection-chez-l-etre-humain</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;</div><div>&bull;\tFatal encephalitis caused by Shamonda virus Europe 1 (SHAV-EU1) in a patient with a severely weakened immune system, France, 2026. <a href=\"https://www.eurosurveillance.org/content/10.2807/1560-7917.ES.2026.31.42.2600883\">https://www.eurosurveillance.org/content/10.2807/1560-7917.ES.2026.31.42.2600883</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;</div><div>&bull;\tMinistry of agriculture, agri-food and food sovereignty, France: Shamonda virus: a first detection in humans. <a href=\"https://agriculture.gouv.fr/virus-shamonda-une-premiere-detection-chez-letre-humain\">https://agriculture.gouv.fr/virus-shamonda-une-premiere-detection-chez-letre-humain</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</div><div>&bull;\tMinistry of agriculture, agri-food and food sovereignty, France: Cases of orthobunyavirus infection in dairy cows. <a href=\"https://agriculture.gouv.fr/cas-dinfections-de-vaches-laitieres-par-un-orthobunyavirus\">https://agriculture.gouv.fr/cas-dinfections-de-vaches-laitieres-par-un-orthobunyavirus</a>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</div><div>&bull;\tPublic Health Agency (Sant&eacute; publique France) France. <a href=\"https://www.santepubliquefrance.fr/les-actualites/virus-shamonda-un-premier-cas-humain-detecte-en-france-que-sait\">https://www.santepubliquefrance.fr/les-actualites/virus-shamonda-un-premier-cas-humain-detecte-en-france-que-sait</a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</div><div>&bull;\tHealth Food chain safety environment, Belgium: Shamonda-like virus: information and frequently asked questions. <a href=\"https://www.health.belgium.be/fr/outils/shamonda-virus-informations-questions-frequentes\">https://www.health.belgium.be/fr/outils/shamonda-virus-informations-questions-frequentes</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;</div><div>&bull;\tSwiss federal authorities, Institute of virology and immunology: Shamonda virus in Switzerland. <a href=\"https://www.ivi.admin.ch/en/neues-orthobunyavirus-der-simbu-serogruppe-bei-rindern-entdeckte\">https://www.ivi.admin.ch/en/neues-orthobunyavirus-der-simbu-serogruppe-bei-rindern-entdeckte</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</div><div>&bull;\tUSCDC: Emergence of Shamonda Orthobunyavirus, Central Europe, 2026. <a href=\"https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/32/12/26-1496_article\">https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/32/12/26-1496_article</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</div><div>&bull;\tPlateforme ESA, France. <a href=\"https://plateforme-esa.fr/fr/un-nouveau-virus-apparente-au-virus-schmallenberg-agent-causal-potentiel-de-cas-epizootiques-de\">https://plateforme-esa.fr/fr/un-nouveau-virus-apparente-au-virus-schmallenberg-agent-causal-potentiel-de-cas-epizootiques-de</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</div><div>&bull;\tEuropean Food Safety Authority (EFSA): Identification and mapping of potential and highly likely vectors for selected vector-borne diseases in the EU and neighbouring countries. <a href=\"https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2026.10061\">https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2026.10061</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;</div><div>&bull;\tWOAH, Schmallenberg virus. <a href=\"https://www.woah.org/app/uploads/2021/03/a-schmallenberg-virus.pdf\">https://www.woah.org/app/uploads/2021/03/a-schmallenberg-virus.pdf</a>&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</div><div><p>&bull;\tWOAH, Bunyaviral disease of animals. <a href=\"https://www.woah.org/fileadmin/Home/eng/Health_standards/tahm/3.10.01_BUNYAVIRAL_DISEASES.pdf\">https://www.woah.org/fileadmin/Home/eng/Health_standards/tahm/3.10.01_BUNYAVIRAL_DISEASES.pdf</a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><strong style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#3c4245;font-size:16px;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">Citable reference:</strong><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#3c4245;font-family:'Noto Sans', Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:16px;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\"> World Health Organization (1 November 2026). Disease Outbreak News; Shamonda virus in human, France. Available at:&nbsp; <a href=\"https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON623\">https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON623</a>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</span></p></div>","Summary":"On 20 October 2026, the National IHR Focal Point (NFP) of France notified WHO regarding the first documented human infection with Shamonda virus (SHAV). The case was an immunocompromised older man from eastern France with no travel history during the preceding year. He developed severe neurological illness on 23 September 2026 and was diagnosed with severe encephalitis (brain inflammation). The patient died on 8 October 2026. \r\nSHAV is recognized primarily as an animal-associated virus, like the closely related Schmallenberg virus circulating in Europe, with no previously documented human infections.\r\nSHAV was confirmed by metagenomic sequencing and reverse transcription polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR), with high viral loads detected in tissue samples collected after death and other causes of encephalitis excluded. There is currently widespread SHAV circulation in animals in Europe, including outbreaks across much of France. The source and route of human infection remain unknown. While vector-borne transmission is most likely, food-borne transmission is under investigation. As this is the first documented laboratory confirmed human infection, WHO considers that the public health risk cannot yet be reliably assessed and recommends continued One Health surveillance and investigation.","PublicationDateAndTime":"2026-11-02T19:00:00Z","TitleSuffix":"","UseOverrideTitle":true,"Title":"Shamonda virus in human - France","Epidemiology":"<p>SHAV belongs to the family <em>Peribunyaviridae</em>, genus <em>Orthobunyavirus</em>, within the Simbu serogroup. It belongs to the same species as Schmallenberg virus (ie <em>Orthobunyavirus schmallenbergense</em>). It was first isolated from cattle in Nigeria in the 1960s and has subsequently been identified in animals in parts of Africa, Japan and Europe.&nbsp;</p><p>Until the recently reported human case, SHAV was recognized primarily as an animal-associated virus, like the closely related Schmallenberg virus circulating in Europe, with no documented human infections. As this is the first confirmed human infection reported globally, there is currently insufficient evidence to assess the extent to which SHAV infects humans or causes disease.</p><p>SHAV was detected for the first time in Europe during the summer of 2026 with confirmed or suspected infections reported in cattle, horses and alpacas in Belgium, France, Germany, the Netherlands and Switzerland. As of 21 October 2026, additional European countries and territories have reported SHAV detections in animals: Austria, Ireland, Jersey, Liechtenstein, Portugal, Slovenia and Spain. In metropolitan France, as of 20 October, the available reports indicate animal detections in 54 departments, predominantly in cattle.</p><p>Two distinct variants of SHAV have been identified in animals in Europe: SHAV-EU1, reported in Germany, Austria, France and Switzerland, and SHAV-EU2, identified in Belgium, the Netherlands, northern Germany and northwestern France.&nbsp;</p><p>Based on current knowledge, SHAV is a vector-borne disease and it is not considered to spread directly between animals. It appears to be transmitted primarily by biting midges of the genus <em>Culicoides</em>, although the main vector species remain unknown. <em>Culicoides</em> (midges) are widespread, and climate change may alter their geographical distribution and abundance, potentially affecting the transmission of vector-borne pathogens in Europe, including Simbu serogroup orthobunyaviruses such as Schmallenberg virus, as well as orbiviruses like bluetongue and African horse sickness viruses. Furthermore, wind-assisted dispersal of infected <em>Culicoides</em> over long distances may also contribute to the introduction and spread of midge-borne viruses into previously unaffected areas, although its role in SHAV transmission requires further investigation. Vertical transmission from mother to fetus during gestation in animals can also occur. &nbsp;</p><p>In cattle, reported signs include acute systemic illness, such as fever, diarrhoea, reduced activity or appetite, and reduced milk production. Neurological disease has been observed in horses with SHAV-positive brain tissue. Congenital malformations have occurred during gestational infection with related Simbu serogroup orthobunyaviruses in animals. However, the full effects of SHAV, including recently detected European strains, on pregnancy, reproduction and fetal development in animals require further investigation.&nbsp;</p><p>Given the concurrent circulation of bluetongue virus in Europe and the overlap in clinical signs, veterinary surveillance and laboratory-based differential diagnosis are important. Laboratory confirmation relies on molecular methods, such as RT-PCR, with sequencing or further testing where indicated. No specific antiviral treatment or vaccine is currently available for SHAV; animal-health prevention measures focus on reducing exposure to biting midges, livestock management practices and routine farm biosecurity.&nbsp;</p><p>Given the potential for overlapping neurological or systemic clinical presentations in animals with other viral infections, such as West Nile virus (WNV), retrospective investigations may be warranted among equine cases presenting with clinical signs compatible with SHAV infection that tested negative for WNV. In addition, testing of samples from WNV-positive horses could help assess the occurrence of possible co-infections and better characterize the contribution of SHAV to equine disease. Such investigations would support a more accurate assessment of the epidemiology, clinical impact and geographical distribution of SHAV in the European equine population.</p><p>The emergence of SHAV in Europe warrants continued epidemiological surveillance and assessment by national veterinary authorities and the public health authorities in Europe using a coordinated One Health approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;As of October 2026, SHAV was not included in the World Organisation for Animal Health (WOAH) list of reportable diseases; however, where SHAV meets the definition of an emerging disease, veterinary authorities are required to notify WOAH in accordance with Article 1.1.5 of the Terrestrial Health Code.&nbsp;</p>","OverrideTitle":"Shamonda virus in human - France","Advice":"<p>In view of the limited evidence on the zoonotic potential of SHAV, the following actions are recommended:</p><ul><li>Strengthened surveillance and information sharing. Continue animal surveillance for SHAV and other orthobunyavirus and consider appropriate human surveillance such as serosurveys, with timely information sharing between veterinary and public health authorities. Suspected human infections should be investigated in accordance with national procedures and available diagnostic capacity.</li><li>Further investigation of the circumstances surrounding the reported case should seek to clarify the source of infection, including the potential contribution of vector exposure.</li><li>Assessment of the potential for infection across different population groups. Further analysis of viral genotype and phenotype is required to better understand its potential for transmission to humans. </li><li>Should additional human cases be identified, investigations should aim to characterize clinical presentation and outcomes, taking account of immune status and relevant underlying conditions or treatments. </li><li>Improved diagnostic and epidemiological understanding, including laboratory investigation and characterization of any suspected cases. &nbsp;SHAV should be considered and tested for in people with relevant symptoms when occupational and/or environmental exposure may be compatible, until the risk to humans is better understood. Collaboration between human and animal health services can help identify possible epidemiological links and improve understanding of the virus's transmission pathways. </li><li>Strengthened entomological investigations. Entomological studies should be enhanced to identify the <em>Culicoides</em> species and potentially other vectors involved in SHAV transmission and assess their geographical distribution, seasonal dynamics and vector competence. Improved understanding of the vectors involved would support both animal and public health risk assessments and help inform surveillance prevention and control strategies.</li><li>Promoting One Health investigations to better characterize SHAV circulation. Investigations in animal populations, including species that may serve as sentinels of viral circulation, can provide valuable information on the geographical spread, transmission dynamics and clinical impact of SHAV. Improved understanding of animal infections may help identify areas of possible human exposure and support public health risk assessment.</li><li>Maintaining proportionate risk communication. Communication should clearly distinguish the established circulation of SHAV in animals from the still-limited evidence of human infection.</li><li>Review the risk assessment as evidence evolves. The assessment should be updated if additional human infections are identified, the route of transmission is clarified, or new evidence emerges regarding the clinical consequences of infection in immunocompromised or immunocompetent individuals.&nbsp;</li></ul><p>Continued surveillance, targeted investigation and coordination between human and animal health authorities are warranted to address uncertainties and inform any subsequent adjustment of public health risk assessment and guidance.&nbsp;</p>","Assessment":"<p>This first documented human infection with SHAV in 2026 demonstrates that human infection is possible. However, a single case is insufficient to assess the risk to the general population, including immunocompetent individuals, or to determine the frequency, clinical spectrum or severity of human infection. The route of infection and possible sources of exposure are unknown, though vector-borne transmission is a plausible hypothesis. </p><p>Although transmission to humans by <em>Culicoides</em> has been documented for Oropouche virus, another orthobunyavirus, currently, there is no evidence that SHAV is transmitted to humans by this route. Although widespread animal infections in Europe justify further investigation of potential human exposure, they do not establish the likelihood of human infection or sustained transmission. Seasonal declines in <em>Culicoides</em> activity may reduce vector-borne transmission in the short term in northern climates, but do not exclude other routes or future exposure.&nbsp; </p><p>Given the very limited data regarding the zoonotic potential of SHAV, the public health risk remains uncertain and cannot yet be reliably assessed or quantified. Continued surveillance, investigation of potential transmission routes and potential new human cases, assessment of clinical outcomes across different population groups and retrospective studies in animal populations are warranted. The risk assessment should be updated as further evidence becomes available.&nbsp;</p>","Overview":"<p>On 20 October 2026, the National IHR Focal Point of France notified WHO of a laboratory-confirmed human infection with Shamonda virus. The case was an immunocompromised older adult male who was hospitalized on 18 August 2026 for his underlying illness. He developed neurological signs on 23 September, including visual disturbances, confusion, loss of consciousness and seizures. A diagnosis of severe encephalitis was made on 25 September, and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) samples were collected. He died on 8 October.</p><p>Virological findings of SHAV were identified by metagenomics on the CSF samples from 25 September, and the French health authorities were notified on 7 October. The sequence belonged to the SHAV-EU1 genotype and is 99% genomically identical to a virus circulating in neighbouring Switzerland, and the sequence retrieved covered approximately 90% of the genome. Retrospective testing of serum samples collected in early July through October, as well as faecal, urine, saliva and respiratory samples, confirmed the presence of SHAV RNA by RT-PCR. Post-mortem biopsies of the brain, liver and spleen showed very high viral genomic loads. A wide panel of tests for other encephalitis pathogens returned negative results. The patient lived in rural eastern France, where animal SHAV transmission has been documented since July 2026, and had not travelled in the preceding year. The patient received multiple blood transfusions between February and October 2026 for his underlying illness. </p><p>Testing performed by the French Blood Establishment (EFS) and the National Reference Center for Arboviruses (CNR) on blood donors linked to transfused red blood cell units was negative for Shamonda virus, making transfusion-associated transmission unlikely, although it cannot be completely excluded at this stage. Epidemiological investigations around the case have not identified a specific source of exposure, although mosquito exposure was reported. Serological investigations are also being conducted among close contacts of the patient.</p><p>Although foodborne transmission is currently considered unlikely, investigations are ongoing to assess the potential excretion of Shamonda virus in the milk of infected cattle and to better characterize any potential implications for food safety. In parallel, veterinary and entomological investigations are continuing to further clarify the vector(s), the ecology, transmission pathways, and public health significance of the virus.</p><p>This is the first laboratory confirmed human case of Shamonda virus disease reported globally. No additional human cases have been detected to date.</p>","DonId":"2026-DON623","Provider":"dynamicProvider372"},{"Id":"f71dec3a-9f8a-4eb1-810c-baeefc888535","LastModified":"2026-10-23T17:14:16Z","PublicationDate":"2026-10-23T15:48:48Z","DateCreated":"2026-10-23T15:48:48Z","IncludeInSitemap":true,"SystemSourceKey":null,"UrlName":"2026-DON622","ItemDefaultUrl":"/2026-DON622","Response":"<p>For detailed information about the ongoing public health response actions by the Ministry of Health, WHO and partners please refer to the latest situation reports published by the WHO Regional Office for Africa: <a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a>. </p><p><strong>Democratic Republic of the Congo</strong></p><p>Health authorities in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in collaboration with WHO and partners, are continuing to implement and coordinate extensive public health measures. These include disease surveillance; laboratory testing; IPC; clinical care; community engagement; research; logistics; and support for response interventions and essential health services, including for the ongoing concurrent humanitarian crisis; engaging donors and mobilizing additional resources to address critical funding gaps and sustain response operations across affected and at-risk areas. A substantial scale-up is ongoing across all response pillars to get ahead of the outbreak. </p><p>On 23 October, the&nbsp;<a href=\"https://cepi.net/first-participant-vaccinated-pivotal-phase-23-bundibugyo-vaccine-trial-drc\">TUMAINI</a> vaccine trial was launched, with first participants enrolled in Bunia. TUMAINI is a pivotal Phase 2/3 clinical trial of vaccine candidates against Bundibugyo virus (BDBV). The trial is an important step towards generating the evidence needed to better protect people against BVD and prevent future outbreaks. WHO is actively supporting the trial, along with partners, with technical, scientific and operational expertise.</p><p><strong>Kenya</strong></p><p>Health authorities in Kenya have implemented safe and dignified burial measures following the BVD-related death in Nairobi, while 135 contacts are currently under quarantine. Kenya has maintained a high level of Ebola preparedness since the outbreak began in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in May 2026; as of 22 October, 709 093 travellers have been screened, 461 samples tested across five laboratories. Kenyan authorities are also coordinating with counterparts in Uganda and the Democratic Republic of the Congo to establish the individual&rsquo;s movements in Uganda and implement any necessary public health measures.</p><p><strong>Nigeria</strong></p><p>The National and State incident management system has been activated by the Nigeria Centre for Disease Control and Prevention to coordinate response.&nbsp; WHO and partners are working closely with health authorities to strengthen control measures, including case investigation, contact tracing, infection prevention and control, and provision of essential supplies including personal protective equipment for health workers. Nigeria has maintained a high level of alert for BVD since the outbreak began in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in May 2026.&nbsp;</p>","FurtherInformation":"<p><strong>Additional information</strong></p><p><strong>Current outbreak: declarations and status</strong></p><ul><li>Official public notification of the Federal Government of Nigeria: <a href=\"https://health.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Immediate-Press-statememnt_confirmed-Ebola-in-Nigeria-1.pdf\">Immediate-Press-statememnt_confirmed-Ebola-in-Nigeria-1.pdf</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/nigeria/news/nigeria-confirms-imported-case-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease\">Nigeria confirms imported case of Ebola Bundibugyo virus disease | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a></li><li>Official public notification of the Ministry of Health of Kenya: <a href=\"https://x.com/MOH_Kenya/status/2107407663231558024\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://x.com/moh_kenya/status/2107407663231558024\">Ministry of Health (@MOH_Kenya) on X</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/05-06-2026-africa-cdc-and-who-launch-joint-continental-ebola-response-plan\">Africa CDC and WHO launch joint continental Ebola response plan</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/publications/bundibugyo-ebola-virus-continental-preparedness-and-response-plan-june-november-2026\">Bundibugyo Ebola virus | Continental preparedness and response plan: June-November 2026</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern\">Epidemic of Ebola Disease caused by Bundibugyo virus in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda determined a public health emergency of international concern</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://administration.sante.gouv.cd/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Declaration-de-la-17e-Epidemie-de-la-maladie-a-virus-Ebola-dans-les-zones-de-sante-de-Rwampara-Mongwalu-et-Bunia-dans-la-province-dIturi.pdf\">The Ministry of Public Health, Hygiene and Social Welfare, DRC, officially declares the 17th Ebola Disease outbreak</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/democratic-republic-congo-confirms-new-ebola-outbreak-who-scales-upsupport\">WHO Democratic Republic of Congo confirms new Ebola outbreak</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/28-05-2026-message-by-the-who-director-general-to-the-people-of-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo\">Message by the WHO Director-General to the people of the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/frontline-trust-day-julienne-anoko-whos-ebola-community-engagement-officer-drc\">At the frontline of trust: a day with Julienne Anoko WHO's Ebola Community Engagement Officer in DRC</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/decentralized-testing-speeds-ebola-response-democratic-republic-congo\">Decentralized testing speeds up Ebola response in the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/uganda/news/uganda-ends-ebola-outbreak-following-completion-42-day-countdown\">Uganda ends Ebola outbreak following completion of 42-day countdown | WHO | Regional Office for Afri&hellip;</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\">Second meeting of the IHR Emergency Committee on the epidemic of Ebola Bundibugyo virus disease in the Democratic Republic of the Congo &ndash; Temporary recommendations</a></li></ul><p><strong style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">WHO Rapid risk assessments</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment-ebola-disease-caused-by-bundibugyo-virus--democratic-republic-of-the-congo-v4\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment-Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo v4</a></li></ul><p><strong style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">Epidemiological updates and situation reports</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Weekly External Situation Report. EBOLA BUNDIBUGYO VIRUS DISEASE OUTBREAK Democratic Republic of the Congo </a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/ebola/situation-summary/index.html\">Ebola Outbreak: Current Situation | Ebola | CDC</a></li><li><a href=\"https://insp.cd/category/sitrep/\">Daily situation report, Ministry of Health, Democratic Republic of the Congo.</a></li></ul><p><strong style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">Published Disease Outbreak News (current outbreak)</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON614\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 1 August 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON615\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 14 August 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON616\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 28 August 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON617\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 11 September 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/india/home/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON618\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 25 September 2026</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON619\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 8 October 2026</a></li></ul><p>&nbsp;<strong>Clinical management, IPC, and occupational safety</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/B09774\">WHO guidelines for the clinical management of filovirus disease</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240111332\">Infection prevention and control guideline for Ebola and Marburg disease. WHO.17 May 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240107205\">Infection prevention and control and water, sanitation and hygiene in health facilities during Ebola or Marburg disease outbreaks: rapid assessment tool, user guide.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240107328\">Assessment and management of health and care workers with possible occupational exposures to Orthoebolavirus or Orthomarburgvirus: implementation guidance</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241515894\">Optimized Supportive Care for Ebola Virus Disease. Clinical management standard operating procedures. WHO. 2019</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240032729\">Framework and toolkit for infection prevention and control in outbreak preparedness, readiness and response at the national level</a></li><li><a href=\"https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/386711\">Diagnostic testing for Ebola disease and Marburg virus disease: interim guidance, 9 July 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/considerations-for-border-health-and-points-of-entry-for-filovirus-disease-outbreaks\">Considerations for border health and points of entry for filovirus disease outbreaks</a></li></ul><p><strong>&nbsp;</strong><strong>Vaccines</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/B09865\">Third meeting of the WHO Technical Advisory Group on candidate vaccine prioritization (TAG-CVP) for Bundibugyo virus disease outbreak response: meeting report, 31 July 2026</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\">&nbsp; </span></li><li>WHO press release regarding a clinical trial to find effective treatments against Bundibugyo virus disease.</li></ul><p><strong>&nbsp;</strong><strong>Training</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/26-03-2025-who-launches-online-training-to-strengthen-filovirus-outbreak-response\">WHO Launches Online Training to Strengthen Filovirus Outbreak Response</a></li></ul><p><strong>Prior Bundibugyo virus disease events, DRC (2012)</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2012_09_14-en\">Disease Outbreak News. Ebola outbreak in Democratic Republic of Congo &ndash; update. WHO. 14 September 2012</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2012_10_26-en\">Disease Outbreak News. Ebola outbreak in Democratic Republic of Congo &ndash; update. WHO. 26 October 2012</a></li></ul><p><strong>Background and reference</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ebola-disease\">Ebola disease fact sheet</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/ebola-vaccines\">Ebola vaccines</a></li></ul><p><strong>Citable reference:</strong> World Health Organization (23 October 2026). Disease Outbreak News; Bundibugyo Virus Disease, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Kenya and Nigeria. Available at: <a href=\"https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON622\">https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON622</a></p>","Summary":"Since the publication of the previous Disease Outbreak News on 8 October 2026, the Bundibugyo virus disease (BVD) outbreak has expanded geographically in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, as well as in the African Region, with Kenya and Nigeria reporting imported cases. \r\nSustained, high-level transmission continues in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. As of 20 October 2026, a total of 9704 confirmed cases, including 4687 deaths (crude case fatality ratio: 48.3%), have been reported across 66 health zones in eight provinces.  The high proportion of deaths occurring in communities continues to highlight challenges in early case detection and timely access to appropriate care. Of the 482 deaths reported since the previous update, 307 occurred in communities and 175 in treatment centres.\r\nOn 6 October 2026, the Ministry of Health of Kenya reported the first laboratory-confirmed imported case of BVD in Kenya in an individual who had travelled from the Democratic Republic of the Congo to Kenya via Uganda. As of 20 October 2026, a total of nine cases of BVD including one death, has been reported in Kenya. The eight additional cases are all known contacts of the first imported case. There is currently no evidence of wider community transmission. \r\nOn 20 October 2026, Nigeria confirmed an imported case in the Federal Capital Territory Abuja in a patient who had travelled from Uganda to the country. The patient is currently in isolation and receiving medical care. Further investigations are ongoing to better understand the complete travel history and the possible source of exposure. \r\nThese developments underscore the need for sustained readiness in the region, including cross-border surveillance, points of entry monitoring, timely case detection and coordinated preparedness and response measures.\r\nWHO advises against any restriction of travel to, or trade with, affected countries. \r\n","PublicationDateAndTime":"2026-10-23T15:03:45Z","TitleSuffix":"","UseOverrideTitle":true,"Title":"Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Kenya and Nigeria","Epidemiology":"<p>Bundibugyo virus disease (BVD) is a severe Ebola disease caused by the Bundibugyo virus, one of the <em>Orthoebolavirus </em>species. It is a zoonotic disease, with fruit bats suspected to be the natural reservoir.</p><p>Human infection is thought to initially occur through close contact with the blood or secretions of infected wildlife, such as bats or non-human primates, and it subsequently spreads from person-to-person through direct contact with the blood, secretions, organs, or other bodily fluids of infected individuals or contaminated surfaces and materials. Transmission is particularly amplified in health-care settings when infection prevention and control (IPC) measures are inadequate, and during unsafe burial practices involving direct contact with deceased individuals.</p><p>The incubation period for BVD ranges from two to 21 days, and infected individuals are not infectious until symptom onset. Early symptoms such as fever, fatigue, muscle pain, headache, and sore throat are non-specific, which complicates clinical diagnosis and can delay detection. These symptoms then progress to gastrointestinal symptoms, organ dysfunction, and, in some cases, haemorrhagic manifestations.</p><p>The CFR from the previous two BVD outbreaks, reported in Uganda and in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in 2007 and 2012, were 30% and 50%, respectively.</p><p>Differentiating BVD from other endemic febrile illnesses such as malaria is challenging without laboratory confirmation. Outbreak control relies on rapid case identification and investigation, isolation and care, contact tracing, safe burials and strong community engagement, as no approved vaccines or specific treatments currently exist for BVD.</p><p>Since first detected in May 2026, this BVD outbreak has rapidly evolved into a large and geographically expanding epidemic in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, with sustained transmission, high mortality and an increasing risk of further international spread. The current outbreak is the second documented Bundibugyo virus disease outbreak in the country, after the 2012 outbreak, and the largest Ebola disease outbreak ever recorded in the Democratic Republic of the Congo irrespective of virus species. The population at greatest risk of exposure is concentrated in communities living in and moving through the health areas with active transmission.&nbsp;</p>","OverrideTitle":"Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo, Kenya and Nigeria","Advice":"<p></p><p>Based on the currently available information, WHO advises against any restriction of travel to, or trade with, affected countries. </p><p>The updated <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\">Temporary Recommendations</a> issued to States Parties on 24 August 2026 underscore the importance of coordinated outbreak control, strengthened cross\u2011border collaboration, and sustained surveillance and preparedness to prevent further regional spread and ensure an effective public health response. Rapid recognition of cases, testing and optimized supportive care can reduce mortality, and improve community perceptions and acceptance of health care within the response.<br /></p> <p></p>","Assessment":"<p>On 14 August 2026, WHO reassessed the risk of the outbreak of BVD. The risk for countries sharing land borders with the Democratic Republic of the Congo was separated from the risk for other countries in the African Region. </p><p>The risk in the Democratic Republic of the Congo was assessed as very high, the risk for countries sharing land borders with the Democratic Republic of the Congo was assessed as high, and the risks for the rest of the African region and at the global level was again assessed as low. For further information, please see <a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment-ebola-disease-caused-by-bundibugyo-virus--democratic-republic-of-the-congo-v4\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment-Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo v4</a>.</p><p>In light of the evolving situation and newly available information, the rapid risk assessment is currently being revised to reassess the risk in affected countries, the African region and globally. &nbsp;</p>","Overview":"<p>Since the previous <a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON619\">Disease Outbreak News</a> was published on 8 October 2026, additional confirmed cases and deaths have been reported in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. </p><p>Cases have been reported in five countries since the declaration of the outbreak on 15 May 2026: the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Uganda, France, and more recently, Kenya and Nigeria. Nigeria is the fifth country affected by the ongoing outbreak, highlighting the continued risk of cross-border transmission. &nbsp;</p><p><strong>Democratic Republic of the Congo</strong></p><p>An additional 976 confirmed cases, including 482 confirmed deaths, have been reported in the Democratic Republic of the Congo since the previous <a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON619\">Disease Outbreak News</a> was published. </p><p>In the most recent 24-hour period, as of 20 October, 67 new confirmed cases were reported, with Ituri accounting for 37 cases, Nord Kivu for 29, and Tshopo for one case. No new cases were reported in Bas-U&eacute;l&eacute;, Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute;, Nord Ubangi, Sud Kivu or Sud-Ubangi during that period. </p><p>As of 20 October, cumulative confirmed cases have reached 9704, including 4687 deaths and 2595 recoveries. The overall crude CFR is 48.3%. </p><p>Confirmed cases have now been identified in 66 health zones across eight provinces. Fifty of the 66 affected zones reported at least one new case during the preceding 21 days, while 16 reported no recent cases. Mandima, Beni and Bunia have reported the highest number of cases and deaths in the preceding 21 days. Ituri remains the most extensively affected province, with 28 of 36 health zones reporting cases during this outbreak, followed by Nord Kivu (17/34), Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; (7/13), Tshopo (7/23), Bas-U&eacute;l&eacute; (4/11), Sud Kivu (1/34), Sud-Ubangi (1/16) and Nord-Ubangi (1/11). Nord-Ubangi is the most recently affected province reporting three confirmed cases including one death and Dakwa in Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; is the most recently affected health zone.</p><p><strong>Figure 1. Distribution of cumulative confirmed cases of Bundibugyo virus disease in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, as of 20 October 2026</strong></p><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/geographic-distribution-of-cases.jpg?sfvrsn=a690e1b8_4\" height=\"607\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"Geographic distribution of cases\" width=\"846\" alt=\"Geographical distribution of confirmed BVD cases in DRC\" sf-size=\"123155\" /></p><p>Ituri remains the epicentre, with 7040 cumulative confirmed cases and 3271 deaths. Nord Kivu follows with 2146 confirmed cases and 1199 deaths, and continues to record the highest provincial CFR, at 55.9%. Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; has reported 421 cases and 183 deaths, while Tshopo has recorded 76 cases and 27 deaths. The less-affected provinces continue to report substantially fewer cases, but transmission persists in several of them. The continued occurrence of cases across multiple provinces shows that the outbreak remains geographically active even as intensity varies between health zones.</p><p>Out of 30 130 contacts under follow-up across six provinces, 24 017 were seen in the last 24 hours corresponding to a follow-up rate of 79.7%, up from 77.8% the previous day, but remaining below the required threshold of 95.0%. Two provinces were at or above the 95.0% threshold: Bas-U&eacute;l&eacute; (98.9%; 740/748) and Sud-Ubangi (97.3%; 109/112). Four provinces were below the threshold: Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; (89.9%; 1286/1430), Ituri (92.8% (6382/6879), with 7 contacts becoming suspected cases, Tshopo (83.7%; 1077/12 086) and Nord-Kivu (73.3%; 14 423/19 675), with performance in Nord-Kivu remaining insufficient. Strengthened contact follow-up is required, particularly in provinces below the target, to achieve the minimum threshold of 95%.</p><p><strong>Figure 2: Number of confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, by date of notification, as of 20 October 2026</strong></p><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/number-of-confirmed-cases3b8ed6e2-f478-49c5-910a-b1f33d743192.jpg?sfvrsn=4a4d748e_3\" height=\"617\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"Number of confirmed cases\" width=\"942\" alt=\"Confirmed cases in DRC\" sf-size=\"101756\" /></p><p><strong>Figure 3: Number of deaths among confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in the Democratic Republic of the Congo by date of notification, as of 20 October 2026.&nbsp;</strong></p><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/number-of-deaths-in-drc14d7fc8d-a2ea-46c8-8acc-054e1d80b971.jpg?sfvrsn=1d891eff_3\" height=\"561\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"Number of deaths in DRC\" width=\"827\" alt=\"Confirmed deaths in DRC\" sf-size=\"77454\" /></p><p><strong>Kenya </strong></p><p>On 6 October 2026, the Ministry of Health of Kenya reported its first laboratory-confirmed imported case of BVD in an adult male who had travelled from the Democratic Republic of the Congo via Uganda by road and arrived in Nairobi on 3 October by air. The case had been ill with other comorbidities for approximately one month and received care at several hospitals in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. He was isolated upon arrival at a hospital in Nairobi. Samples collected on 5 October tested positive for Bundibugyo virus at two national laboratories. Despite supportive care, the patient died on 5 October and was given a safe and dignified burial on 6 October. </p><p>As of 20 October 2026, a total of nine cases, including one death, has been reported in Kenya. &nbsp;All eight secondary cases are epidemiologically linked to the imported index case and associated with one cluster. They include four family members and four health and care workers. All new cases were under quarantine at the time of symptom onset and are currently in isolation and receiving appropriate supportive care and treatment at two designated facilities.</p><p>There is currently no evidence of transmission outside this cluster. </p><p>As of 20 October, all of the 135 contacts have been traced and quarantined. </p><p><strong>Figure 4: Number of confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in Kenya, by date of notification, as of 21 October 2026</strong></p><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/number-of-cases-in-kenya.jpg?sfvrsn=375c4518_4\" height=\"356\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"Number of cases in Kenya\" width=\"685\" alt=\"Number of confirmed cases in Kenya\" sf-size=\"27947\" /></p><p><strong>Nigeria</strong></p><p>On 20 October 2026, Nigeria reported one laboratory-confirmed imported case of BVD in the Federal Capital Territory Abuja, becoming the fifth country to detect the virus since the outbreak was declared in the Democratic Republic of the Congo on 15 May 2026. The case developed symptoms on 7 October, four days after arriving in Nigeria, sought care in Abuja on 11 October and tested positive for Bundibugyo virus on 20 October 2026. The case is currently in isolation and receiving medical care. </p><p>As of 20 October, 44 contacts had been identified and are being monitored. National authorities, with support from WHO and partners, have intensified contact tracing, case investigation and infection prevention and control measures, including the provision of essential supplies and personal protective equipment for health workers. Investigations are ongoing to establish the likely source of infection and reconstruct the travel and exposure history. WHO has urged countries across the region to strengthen surveillance, laboratory diagnostic capacity, rapid response readiness and cross-border preparedness to mitigate the risk of further transmission.</p>","DonId":"2026-DON622","Provider":"dynamicProvider372"},{"Id":"eafb07ec-9187-4b39-b310-94b99a89571a","LastModified":"2026-10-16T14:58:58Z","PublicationDate":"2026-10-15T16:22:00Z","DateCreated":"2026-10-15T16:22:00Z","IncludeInSitemap":true,"SystemSourceKey":null,"UrlName":"middle-east-respiratory-syndrome-coronavirus---kingdom-of-saudi-arabia","ItemDefaultUrl":"/middle-east-respiratory-syndrome-coronavirus---kingdom-of-saudi-arabia","Response":"<p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;color:#3c4245;font-family:'Noto Sans', Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:16px;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">The Kingdom of Saudi Arabia has incorporated MERS-CoV testing into sentinel surveillance algorithms, with testing conducted among patients who test negative for influenza and SARS-CoV-2. This approach aims to improve case detection given the overlapping clinical presentation of MERS, COVID-19, influenza and other respiratory infections. Additionally, IPC practices have improved considerably since 2012, implementing regular training for health and care workers, IPC programmes in health facilities and at national level and improving IPC practices in healthcare settings.</span></p><p>National health authorities continue to promote public awareness and risk reduction measures, particularly regarding zoonotic transmission from dromedary camels. Recommended measures include regular hand hygiene before and after animal contact, avoiding contact with sick animals, avoiding consumption of raw camel milk, camel urine, and undercooked camel meat, and seeking medical care when respiratory symptoms develop. The Ministry of Health also continues to promote respiratory hygiene measures, including covering coughs and sneezes, avoiding close contact with ill individuals, and maintaining good respiratory hygiene. In parallel, coordination has been established between the Public Health Authority and the National Center for the Prevention and Control of Plant Pests and Animal Diseases. Appropriate control measures have been implemented for involved camels in accordance with animal health recommendations.</p><p>Contact tracing has been conducted for all confirmed cases; no secondary cases have been identified to date. Enhanced infection prevention and control (IPC) measures remain in place at healthcare facilities. Health and care workers who are exposed before patients are isolated or identified as being infectious, were followed up and no secondary cases have been reported in healthcare facilities as a result of these cases.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>","FurtherInformation":"<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.moh.gov.sa/en/healthawareness/educationalcontent/corona/pages/015.aspx\" style=\"text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;white-space:inherit;\">Ministry of Health of the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, Coronavirus (MERS-CoV)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/health-topics/middle-east-respiratory-syndrome-coronavirus-mers#tab=tab_1\">WHO, &nbsp;Middle East respiratory syndrome coronavirus (MERS-CoV)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/en/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/middle-east-respiratory-syndrome-coronavirus-(mers-cov)\">WHO. Middle East Respiratory Syndrome coronavirus (MERS_CoV), Key facts</a></li><li><a href=\"https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/5102de84-cacb-4eb7-b021-149b90997df5/content\">WHO. MERS global summary and assessment of risk (2026)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/update-88-mers-cov-a-circulating-coronavirus-with-epidemic-and-pandemic-potential-pandemic-preparedness--prevention-and-response-with-a-one-health-approach\">WHO Update 88: MERS-CoV, a circulating coronavirus with epidemic and pandemic potential - Pandemic preparedness, prevention and response with a One Health approach</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.emro.who.int/health-topics/mers-cov/mers-outbreaks.html?format=html\">WHO-EMRO MERS monthly bulletin</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/westernpacific/emergencies/2015-mers-outbreak\">WHO-WPRO MERS outbreak in the Republic of Korea, 2015</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/risk-assessment-of-the-introduction--spread--and-zoonotic-spillover-of-mers-cov-clade-b-in-camel-populations-of-the-nile-basin-and-across-africa\" style=\"text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;white-space:inherit;\">GLEWS+ (FAO-WHO-WOAH) risk assessment of the introduction, spread, and zoonotic spillover of MERS-CoV Clade B in camel populations of the Nile Basin and Across Africa</a></li></ul><p><strong>Citable reference</strong>: World Health Organization (15 October 2026). Disease Outbreak News; Middle East Respiratory Syndrome coronavirus &ndash; Kingdom of Saudi Arabia. Available at:&nbsp; <a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON618\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON618</a></p>","Summary":"Between 1 January and 5 October 2026, the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia reported eight laboratory-confirmed cases of Middle East Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus (MERS-CoV) infection, including one death. Cases were detected across six locations: Al Jouf (1), Arar (1), Hafr Al-Batin (1), Riyadh (2), Tabuk (1) and Taif (2). No epidemiological links were identified between any of the cases.\r\nAll eight cases were symptomatic, most commonly presenting with fever (6/8), dyspnea (5/8), and cough (4/8). None were health and care workers. Infection was confirmed by RT-PCR testing. MERS is a zoonotic viral respiratory disease for which dromedary camels serve as the primary animal reservoir and two cases reported direct contact with camels while six reported no known camel exposure. Seven of eight cases had underlying comorbidities, recognized risk factors for severe MERS-CoV disease. The cases appear to represent sporadic infections related to zoonotic spillover rather than a single outbreak cluster.","PublicationDateAndTime":"2026-10-15T16:00:00Z","TitleSuffix":"","UseOverrideTitle":true,"Title":"Middle East respiratory syndrome coronavirus - Kingdom of Saudi Arabia","Epidemiology":"<p>Middle East respiratory syndrome (MERS) is a respiratory illness caused by a coronavirus (MERS-CoV). The fatality rate among confirmed cases is around 36%, though this may be an overestimate since milder cases often go undetected. The CFR is calculated based solely on laboratory-confirmed infections, which may not reflect the correct mortality rate.</p><p>Humans contract MERS-CoV through direct or indirect contact with dromedary camels, the virus&rsquo;s natural host and zoonotic reservoir. Human-to-human transmission is possible and occurs predominantly among close contacts (family and household members) and in health-care settings (health-care workers and among patients). The largest outbreaks occurred in health-care facilities in the Republic of Korea, Saudi Arabia, and the United Arab Emirates. Outside these environments, there has been limited documented human-to-human transmission to date.</p><p>MERS can present with no symptoms, mild respiratory issues, or severe illness leading to acute respiratory distress and death. Common symptoms include fever, cough, and breathing difficulties, with pneumonia frequently observed, though not always present. Some patients also experience gastrointestinal symptoms such as diarrhoea. Severe cases may require intensive care, including mechanical ventilation. Those at higher risk of severe outcomes include older adults, individuals with weakened immune systems, and those with chronic conditions like diabetes, kidney disease, cancer, or lung disorders.</p><p>No vaccine or specific treatment is currently available, although several MERS-CoV-specific vaccines and therapeutics are in development. Treatment remains supportive, focusing on managing symptoms based on the severity of the illness.</p>","OverrideTitle":"Middle East respiratory syndrome coronavirus - Kingdom of Saudi Arabia","Advice":"<p>Based on the current\r\nsituation and available information, WHO re-emphasizes the importance of\r\nmaintaining strong surveillance for acute respiratory infections in all Member\r\nStates. Where warranted by the epidemiological context, MERS-CoV should be\r\nincluded in the testing algorithm and included in differential diagnosis for\r\npatients presenting with acute respiratory illness and reporting history of\r\ntravel to affected areas, and any unusual patterns of respiratory illness or\r\nsevere acute respiratory infections should be carefully reviewed.<o:p></o:p></p><p>Human-to-human\r\ntransmission of MERS-CoV in healthcare settings has been associated with delays\r\nin recognizing acute respiratory symptoms of MERS-CoV infection as they are\r\noften non-specific and similar to other respiratory illnesses, leading to\r\ndelayed triage of suspected cases and delayed implementation of IPC measures &mdash;\r\nboth critical to prevent the spread of MERS-CoV in healthcare facilities and if\r\nleft unmanaged have led to large healthcare outbreaks (example of the Republic of Korea\r\nin 2015). Health and care workers should consistently apply standard\r\nprecautions for all patients at every interaction in healthcare settings. In\r\naddition, transmission-based precautions should be implemented when caring for\r\npatients with symptoms of acute respiratory infection who are suspected of\r\nhaving a viral infection, including suspected, probable or confirmed MERS-CoV\r\ninfection. Contact and droplet precautions should be applied, while airborne\r\nprecautions should be implemented when performing aerosol-generating\r\nprocedures. Early identification, case management and prompt isolation of\r\ncases, contact tracing, together with appropriate IPC measures in healthcare\r\nsettings and public health awareness, can reduce the risk of human-to-human\r\ntransmission of MERS-CoV.<o:p></o:p></p><p>MERS-CoV appears to\r\ncause more severe disease in people with underlying chronic medical conditions\r\nsuch as diabetes, renal failure, chronic lung disease and in immunocompromised\r\npersons. Therefore, people with these underlying medical conditions should avoid\r\nclose contact with dromedary camels or their products, particularly when\r\nvisiting farms, markets, barns or other areas where the virus may be\r\ncirculating.<o:p></o:p></p><p>General hygiene\r\nmeasures should be followed when interacting with animals or animal products,\r\nincluding regular hand washing before and after touching animals and avoiding\r\ncontact with sick animals.<o:p></o:p></p><p>Appropriate food\r\nhygiene should be observed when handling or consuming camel products. People\r\nshould avoid drinking raw camel milk or camel urine or consuming meat that has\r\nnot been properly cooked.<o:p></o:p></p><p>WHO does not advise\r\nspecial screening at points of entry in relation to this event, nor does it\r\ncurrently recommend the application of any travel or trade restrictions.&nbsp;</p>","Assessment":"<p>MERS is a zoonotic viral respiratory disease caused by MERS-CoV, for which dromedary camels serve as the primary animal reservoir. Human infection can range from asymptomatic or mild illness to severe disease and death. Human infections continue to occur through direct or indirect exposure to infected dromedaries or their products, while limited human-to-human transmission has been documented, primarily among close contacts and in healthcare settings. Sustained community transmission has not been observed. No licensed vaccine for MERS-CoV exists to date.</p><p>As of 5 October 2026, WHO had been notified of 2643 laboratory-confirmed MERS cases globally, including 965 deaths, since the virus was first identified in 2012. The majority of cases (84%; 2232 cases and 870 deaths) have been reported from Saudi Arabia. These figures likely underestimate the true number of infections since mild and asymptomatic cases may remain undetected/unreported, while the reported case fatality of ratio of 37% may overestimate mortality among infections.</p><p>Although the number of reported MERS cases has declined substantially since 2020, MERS-CoV continues to circulate in dromedary camel populations in the Middle East and African regions and continues to cause sporadic human infections. The observed decline in reported MERS cases, in particular during the COVID-19 pandemic emergency phase, is thought to be a result of pandemic-related IPC measures that also limited human-to-human transmission of MERS-CoV, as well as behavioral changes during the pandemic. Any role of potential cross-reactive immunity from SARS-CoV-2 infection and/ or vaccination remains in need of further investigation. Other hypotheses&mdash;such as reduced surveillance, viral attenuation, or decreased circulation in camel populations&mdash;are not supported by current evidence.</p><p>WHO expects additional sporadic MERS cases to occur in countries where the virus circulates in camels and anticipates that travel-associated exportation of cases will continue. Delays in detection remain possible due to the clinical presentation of MERS being similar with other respiratory infections, including influenza and COVID-19. Numerous outbreaks <a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/12-june-2015-mers-korea-en\">in KSA and the Republic of Korea</a> have demonstrated that primary cases can result in substantial secondary transmission, particularly in healthcare settings and through importation of travel related cases.</p><p>The notification of the recent cases in Saudi Arabia does not change WHO's overall risk assessment - &nbsp;the risk remains moderate at both the regional and global levels, reflecting the continued occurrence of sporadic zoonotic infections, the potential for healthcare-associated outbreaks and international exportation of cases, in the absence of sustained human-to-human transmission. </p><p>WHO continues to monitor the epidemiological situation through routine surveillance and reporting under the International Health Regulations (IHR 2005) and conducts ongoing risk assessments using available epidemiological and virological information.</p>","Overview":"<p>Between 1 January and 5 October 2026, a total of eight cases of MERS-CoV, including one death, was reported from six locations in the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia. Of the eight reported cases, five occurred among persons aged 70 years and older, two among adults aged 50&ndash;59 years, and one among an adult aged 30&ndash;39 years. Seven cases had underlying comorbidities, two reported direct camel contact, and one death was reported in an older adult (&gt;65 year old) with comorbidities. There was no known epidemiological link identified among cases. </p><p><strong>Case 1: </strong>A male aged 50&ndash;59 years, with comorbidities, from Al Jouf developed symptoms on 14 March 2026. He reported no known contact with camels or camel products. The patient was hospitalized on 17 March and laboratory confirmation of MERS-CoV infection was obtained by RT-PCR on 19 March.<strong> </strong><strong></strong></p><p><strong>Case 2: </strong>A male aged &ge;70 years, with comorbidities, from Taif developed symptoms on 18 April 2026. He reported no known contact with camels or camel products. The patient was hospitalized on the same day, and laboratory confirmation was obtained on 22 April. He subsequently died as a result of the illness.<strong></strong></p><p><strong>Case 3:</strong><strong> </strong>A male aged &ge;70 years, with comorbidities, from Hafr Al-Batin developed symptoms on 20 May 2026. He reported direct contact with camels. The patient was hospitalized on 26 May and laboratory confirmation was obtained on 27 May.<strong> </strong></p><p><strong>Case 4:</strong> A female aged &ge;70 years, <strong>with comorbidities,</strong> from Riyadh developed symptoms on 31 July 2026. No known exposure to camels or camel products was reported. She was hospitalized on 3 August and laboratory confirmation was obtained on 8 August. <strong></strong></p><p><strong>Case 5:</strong> A male aged 50&ndash;59 years, <strong>with comorbidities,</strong> from Tabuk developed symptoms on 20 August 2026. He reported no known contact with camels or camel products. He was hospitalized on 23 August and laboratory confirmation was obtained by RT-PCR on 24 August. </p><p><strong>Case 6:</strong>&nbsp;A male aged 30&ndash;39 years, with no known comorbidities, from Riyadh developed symptoms on 14 August 2026. He reported no known contact with camels or camel products. He was hospitalized on 20 August and laboratory confirmation was obtained on 23 August. </p><p><strong>Case 7:</strong> A male aged &ge;70 years, <strong>with comorbidities,</strong> from Taif developed symptoms on 11 September 2026. He reported no known contact with camels or camel products. He was hospitalized on 17 September and laboratory confirmation was obtained on 19 September. </p><p><strong>Case 8:</strong> A male aged 60&ndash;69 years, <strong>with comorbidities,</strong> from Arar developed symptoms on 20 September 2026. He reported direct contact with camels. He was hospitalized on 20 September and laboratory confirmation was obtained on 21 September.&nbsp;<br /></p>","DonId":"2026-DON618","Provider":"dynamicProvider372"},{"Id":"9cca163d-6ef3-4d3e-b149-2cd7acf408f6","LastModified":"2026-10-13T09:22:55Z","PublicationDate":"2026-10-10T15:53:19Z","DateCreated":"2026-10-10T15:53:19Z","IncludeInSitemap":true,"SystemSourceKey":null,"UrlName":"2026-DON620","ItemDefaultUrl":"/2026-DON620","Response":"<p><p></p><p>The NFP of the Russian Federation publicly announced that the regional sanitary and anti-epidemic commission convened an extraordinary meeting on 2 October. RPN, the responsible agency, approved an operational action plan of sanitary and anti-epidemic measures, in accordance with national legislation, including contact tracing and medical follow-up, laboratory testing of identified contacts, enhanced epidemiological investigation and risk assessment and implementation of precautionary infection prevention and control measures. Five Rospotrebnadzor organizations, including three research institutions, were involved in the measures. The preventive and anti-epidemic measures were implemented in accordance with the patient's professional activities. No infectious disease-related health concerns were identified. More than 5500 laboratory tests were conducted, and none detected high-consequence or dangerous pathogenic microorganisms. Testing was performed using a broad range of established laboratory methods, including bacteriological, biological, mass spectrometric, and molecular genetic techniques. No high-threat pathogens were detected among contacts, and all follow-up activities were completed without identifying additional cases.</p><p></p></p>","FurtherInformation":"<ul><li>Rospotrebnadzor: a full range of preventive measures has been completed in Irkutsk. Available here: <a href=\"https://www.rospotrebnadzor.ru/deyatelnost/epidemiological-surveillance/?ELEMENT_ID=33189\">https://www.rospotrebnadzor.ru/deyatelnost/epidemiological-surveillance/?ELEMENT_ID=33189</a></li><li>Shelekhovsky District official website, Extraordinary meeting of the Regional Sanitary and Anti-Epidemic Commission. Available here: <a href=\"https://www.sheladm.ru/p/044cd85d94814bb99845ef58826e6adc\">https://www.sheladm.ru/p/044cd85d94814bb99845ef58826e6adc</a></li><li>Irkutsk region Governor. Available here: &nbsp;<a href=\"https://web.max.ru/-68185301813993\">https://web.max.ru/-68185301813993</a></li><li>Irkutsk Anti-Plague Research Institute of Siberia and Far East. Available here: <a href=\"https://irknipchi.ru/index_eng.htm\">https://irknipchi.ru/index_eng.htm</a></li><li>World Health Organization (2020). Laboratory biosafety manual, 4th ed. World Health Organization. Available here: <a href=\"https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/337956.\">https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/337956.</a></li><li>World Health Organization (2021). WHO guidelines for plague management: revised recommendations for the use of rapid diagnostic tests, fluoroquinolones for case management and personal protective equipment for prevention of post-mortem transmission. Available here: <a href=\"https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/341505\">https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/341505</a>.</li><li>World Health Organization. Fact Sheet Plague. Available here: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/plague\">https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/plague</a></li></ul><strong>Citable reference:</strong> World Health Organization (10 October 2026). Disease Outbreak News; Pneumonia of undetermined etiology in the Russian Federation. Available at:&nbsp; <a href=\"https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON620\">https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON620</a>&nbsp;","Summary":"On 2 October 2026, WHO became aware through media reports of the death of an employee of the Irkutsk Antiplague Research Institute (APRI) of Siberia and the Far East, the Russian Federation. According to these reports, the individual died on 2 October following hospitalization for severe pneumonia on 29 September, and pneumonic plague was suspected as the cause of death, following occupational exposure in laboratory settings. \nFollowing requests for verification from WHO, the National Focal Point for the International Health Regulations (2005) (NFP) of the Russian Federation confirmed the death and informed WHO that no case of plague had been registered recently in Irkutsk Oblast, that preventive and control measures had been implemented in accordance with national sanitary legislation, and that the laboratory testing of contacts detected no high-threat pathogens. The authorities also reported that no biosecurity incidents had occurred in the laboratory, contrary to reports in the media, and that there were no further case of severe pneumonia linked to this case. \nThe NFP informed WHO that the case and all contacts had been tested for a range of microorganisms, including high-threat pathogens. Test results were negative for the tested high-threat pathogens. All contacts had been medically followed up in healthcare facilities or at home, and all remained asymptomatic throughout. Quarantine measures were instituted in the hospital where the patient was being treated. Based on the information provided by NFP, WHO has not identified evidence of ongoing transmission associated with this event. A key uncertainty remains the absence of a confirmed diagnosis in the deceased laboratory worker. \nWHO will continue to monitor the situation and assess any potential public health implications as further information becomes available.\n","PublicationDateAndTime":"2026-10-10T15:00:00Z","TitleSuffix":"","UseOverrideTitle":false,"Title":"Pneumonia of undetermined etiology - the Russian Federation","Epidemiology":"","OverrideTitle":"","Advice":"<p><p></p><p></p><p></p><p>WHO encourages all States Parties to the IHR to actively engage with the WHO IHR Contact Points in their respective WHO Regional Office for timely event-related communications, including by applying, to that effect, the decision instrument contained in Annex 2 of the IHR, intended to inform the decision by national authorities to notify WHO (Article 6) or to consult with WHO (Article 8).</p><p>As the etiology of the fatal illness remains undetermined, further investigation of available clinical specimens and epidemiological information should continue, as appropriate. Additional laboratory testing, including confirmatory testing by reference laboratories where needed, may assist in establishing the causative agent and refining the public health risk assessment.</p><p>Depending on seasonality and local epidemiology, States Parties may consider enhancing or strengthening community-based, event-based or sentinel surveillance for respiratory infections and strengthening testing capacities for respiratory pathogens. Public health authorities may also encourage uptake of recommended vaccinations against seasonal influenza, COVID-19, pneumococcal diseases and other vaccine-preventable respiratory infections as appropriate.</p><p>Given that the reported event involved a laboratory worker and that initial media reports suggested a possible occupational exposure or laboratory-related incident, WHO recalls the importance of maintaining robust biosafety, biosecurity, and infection prevention and control practices in laboratories handling potentially hazardous biological agents. This recommendation is precautionary in nature and does not imply that a laboratory-associated infection or incident has been confirmed in this event.</p><p>Should an occupational exposure to biological hazards occur among personnel working in laboratory settings, regardless of the specific role or the nature of the clinical outcome, WHO recommends strict adherence to appropriate biosafety, biosecurity and infection prevention and control measures in accordance with established protocols. These measures are intended to minimize the risk of occupational exposure to and unintended release of infectious agents and should be applied consistently as part of routine laboratory practice, without implying that a particular infection or clinical condition was necessarily acquired as a result of occupational exposure in the laboratory setting.</p><p>Based on the information currently available, WHO does not advise any restrictions on travel or trade related to this event. WHO will continue monitoring the situation and updating its recommendations as additional information becomes available.</p><p></p><p></p><p></p></p>","Assessment":"<p><p></p><p>WHO&rsquo;s rapid risk assessment is a systematic and continuous process that characterizes the risk posed by a public health event and informs measures to reduce its impact and prevent international spread.</p><p>A key uncertainty for this event is the absence of a confirmed diagnosis in the deceased laboratory worker. According to information provided by the national authorities, testing did not identify plague or other tested high-threat pathogens, and the death has been classified as pneumonia of undetermined etiology. However, WHO has not received information identifying the causative agent responsible for the fatal illness. Given the absence of a confirmed diagnosis, WHO is not in a position to independently assess whether the duration of the reported monitoring period for contacts was sufficient to exclude all plausible causes of the fatal illness.</p><p>The occupational setting of the case remains an important consideration. The deceased individual worked at an institution that handles high-consequence pathogens, and precautionary public health measures were reportedly implemented because of the individual's professional activities. While the information provided by the authorities does not indicate infection with tested high-threat pathogens or identify additional linked cases, the source of the fatal illness remains undetermined. A laboratory-associated exposure therefore cannot be fully excluded at this stage.</p><p>Based on the information provided by NFP, WHO has not identified evidence of ongoing transmission associated with this event. However, confidence in the assessment remains limited by the absence of a confirmed etiological diagnosis, and the remaining uncertainty regarding the source and mode of exposure. Additional clinical, epidemiological and laboratory information, including confirmation of the diagnosis in the deceased laboratory worker, is required to improve confidence in the assessment. WHO will continue monitoring the situation and assess any potential public health implications as further information becomes available.&nbsp;</p><p></p></p>","Overview":"<p>On 2 October 2026, WHO became aware through media reports of the death of an employee of the Irkutsk APRI of Siberia and the Far East, Russian Federation. The individual died on 2 October following hospitalization for severe pneumonia on 29 September.</p><p>On 3 October, 5 October, 6 October and 7 October, WHO contacted the Russian Federation health authorities to verify the information reported by media including a purported second case and in accordance with Articles 9 and 10 of the International Health Regulations (2005) (IHR), WHO also asked for further information to assess the potential international public health implications in accordance with Article 6 of the IHR.&nbsp;</p><p>On 2 October, a public statement issued by the Irkutsk branch of Rospotrebnadzor (RPN), a federal agency that works to provide oversight and control of wellbeing and consumer rights and protection in the Russian Federation, indicated that a &ldquo;special sanitary-epidemiological expert commission&rdquo; had been convened for the suspected case of high-threat pathogen. Sanitary and epidemic control measures were put in place, including identifying contacts who were placed under medical observation. Russian Federation authorities stated at the time that all contacts were without symptoms and with negative laboratory results for high-threat pathogens.</p><p>On 6 October, WHO received a response to its verification request from the NFP of the Russian Federation, stating that no case of plague has been registered recently in Irkutsk Oblast, and confirming that an epidemiological investigation had been conducted following the identification of a case of severe pneumonia in an employee of the Irkutsk APRI. According to authorities, the individual initially presented with symptoms compatible with an acute respiratory viral infection. After testing negative for a number of high-threat pathogens, the patient was determined to have died from pneumonia of undetermined etiology.</p><p>Russian authorities informed WHO of the following: that preventive and control measures had been implemented in accordance with national sanitary legislation; that the widest possible range of contacts were identified, placed under medical observation and tested for high-threat pathogens, and that as of 9 October, no high-threat pathogens within the laboratory&rsquo;s research portfolio had been detected in any of the contacts, and their medical observation has been discontinued. The authorities further reported that no laboratory biosafety or biosecurity incidents had occurred at the institute, as determined by two independent investigations and stated that media reports suggesting otherwise were inaccurate.</p><p>According to the Russian Federation authorities, the situation continues to be monitored.</p><p>Following subsequent requests for information from WHO on 7 and 8 October, the NFP of the Russian Federation provided further information. According to their official report, the patient and all identified contacts were tested for a broad range of pathogens, including influenza, SARS-CoV-2, other respiratory infections, and bacterial infections (including plague and streptococcus). All results were reportedly negative for high-threat pathogens, and only two cases of COVID-19, three cases of rhinovirus, one case of <em>Haemophilus</em> spp., and other opportunistic infections were identified among contacts. The authorities further reported that the fatality was not due to plague infection, had been vaccinated against the high-threat pathogens with which the employee had worked occupationally. The report also stated that the individual had not travelled outside of Irkutsk Oblast during the two weeks prior to symptom onset. &nbsp;</p><p>WHO has offered support in the areas of high-threat pathogens and laboratory biosafety and biosecurity to the public health authorities in the Russian Federation.</p><p>In response to further information about the public health measures put in place, Russian Federation authorities reported that given the patient&rsquo;s occupation, precautionary public health measures were implemented, and a broad range of contacts were identified and placed under medical observation either in healthcare facilities or at home. Medical observation, including daily laboratory testing, was conducted for seven days and has been completed for all identified contacts. All contacts remained asymptomatic and tested negative for the pathogens under investigation.</p><p>The Russian Federation authorities informed WHO that, apart from the hospital where the case was admitted, no other healthcare facility was placed under quarantine or subjected to additional public health measures, contrary to media reports.</p>","DonId":"2026-DON620","Provider":"dynamicProvider372"},{"Id":"8fbb14a7-4f4d-4e4a-b113-68b944028ebf","LastModified":"2026-10-08T16:16:01Z","PublicationDate":"2026-10-08T16:06:26Z","DateCreated":"2026-10-08T16:16:01Z","IncludeInSitemap":true,"SystemSourceKey":null,"UrlName":"2026-DON619","ItemDefaultUrl":"/2026-DON619","Response":"<p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">For detailed information about the ongoing public health response actions by the Ministry of Health, WHO and partners please refer to the latest situation reports published by the WHO Regional Office for Africa: </span><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" style=\"text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;white-space:inherit;\">Ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></p><div><p paraid=\"2108908504\" paraeid=\"{1b66a25d-dc73-4b2c-a10e-a39ed8cbee4c}{7}\">Health authorities in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in collaboration with WHO and partners, are continuing to implement and coordinate extensive public health measures, including disease surveillance, laboratory testing, infection prevention and control, clinical care, community engagement, research, logistics and support for response interventions and essential health services, engaging donors and mobilizing additional resources to address critical funding gaps and sustain response operations across affected and at-risk areas. A substantial scale-up is ongoing across all response pillars to get ahead of the outbreak.&nbsp;</p></div>","FurtherInformation":"<p><strong>Regular Information products on the outbreak o</strong><strong>f Bundibugyo virus disease </strong><strong></strong></p><ul><li><strong>Daily update: </strong><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/alert-and-response\">Epidemiological update on BVD outbreak in Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li><li><strong>Published weekly: </strong><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a> | WHO| Regional Office for Africa </li><li><strong>Published biweekly:&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news\"><strong></strong>Disease Outbreak News | All Hazards Public Health Events, Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li></ul><p><strong>Additional information</strong></p><p><strong style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">Current outbreak: declarations and status</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://afro.who.int/countries/kenya/news/kenya-confirms-first-imported-bundibugyo-virus-disease-case-who-supports-control-efforts\">Kenya confirms first imported Bundibugyo virus disease case; WHO supports control efforts</a></li><li>Official public notification of the Ministry of Health of Kenya: <a href=\"https://x.com/MOH_Kenya/status/2107407663231558024\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Ministry of Health (@MOH_Kenya) on X</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/05-06-2026-africa-cdc-and-who-launch-joint-continental-ebola-response-plan\">Africa CDC and WHO launch joint continental Ebola response plan</a><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/publications/bundibugyo-ebola-virus-continental-preparedness-and-response-plan-june-november-2026\"></a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/publications/bundibugyo-ebola-virus-continental-preparedness-and-response-plan-june-november-2026\">Bundibugyo Ebola virus | Continental preparedness and response plan: June-November 2026</a><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern\"></a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern\">Epidemic of Ebola Disease caused by Bundibugyo virus in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda determined a public health emergency of international concern</a><a href=\"https://administration.sante.gouv.cd/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Declaration-de-la-17e-Epidemie-de-la-maladie-a-virus-Ebola-dans-les-zones-de-sante-de-Rwampara-Mongwalu-et-Bunia-dans-la-province-dIturi.pdf\"></a></li><li><a href=\"https://administration.sante.gouv.cd/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Declaration-de-la-17e-Epidemie-de-la-maladie-a-virus-Ebola-dans-les-zones-de-sante-de-Rwampara-Mongwalu-et-Bunia-dans-la-province-dIturi.pdf\">The Ministry of Public Health, Hygiene and Social Welfare, DRC, officially declares the 17th Ebola Disease outbreak</a><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/democratic-republic-congo-confirms-new-ebola-outbreak-who-scales-upsupport\"></a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/democratic-republic-congo-confirms-new-ebola-outbreak-who-scales-upsupport\">WHO Democratic Republic of Congo confirms new Ebola outbreak</a><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/28-05-2026-message-by-the-who-director-general-to-the-people-of-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo\"></a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/28-05-2026-message-by-the-who-director-general-to-the-people-of-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo\">Message by the WHO Director-General to the people of the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/frontline-trust-day-julienne-anoko-whos-ebola-community-engagement-officer-drc\"></a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/frontline-trust-day-julienne-anoko-whos-ebola-community-engagement-officer-drc\">At the frontline of trust: a day with Julienne Anoko WHO's Ebola Community Engagement Officer in DRC</a><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/decentralized-testing-speeds-ebola-response-democratic-republic-congo\"></a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/decentralized-testing-speeds-ebola-response-democratic-republic-congo\">Decentralized testing speeds up Ebola response in the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/uganda/news/uganda-ends-ebola-outbreak-following-completion-42-day-countdown\"></a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/uganda/news/uganda-ends-ebola-outbreak-following-completion-42-day-countdown\">Uganda ends Ebola outbreak following completion of 42-day countdown | WHO | Regional Office for Afri&hellip;</a><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\"></a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\">Second meeting of the IHR Emergency Committee on the epidemic of Ebola Bundibugyo virus disease in the Democratic Republic of the Congo &ndash; Temporary recommendations</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>WHO Rapid risk assessments</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment-ebola-disease-caused-by-bundibugyo-virus--democratic-republic-of-the-congo-v4\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment-Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo v4</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Epidemiological updates and situation reports</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Weekly External Situation Report. EBOLA BUNDIBUGYO VIRUS DISEASE OUTBREAK Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/ebola/situation-summary/index.html\">Ebola Outbreak: Current Situation | Ebola | CDC</a></li><li><a href=\"https://insp.cd/category/sitrep/\">Daily situation report, Ministry of Health, Democratic Republic of the Congo.</a></li></ul><p><strong>Published Disease Outbreak News (current outbreak)</strong><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\"></span></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON616\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 28 August 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON617\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 10 September 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON618\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 25 September 2026</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Clinical management, IPC, and occupational safety</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/B09774\">WHO guidelines for the clinical management of filovirus disease</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240111332\">Infection prevention and control guideline for Ebola and Marburg disease. WHO.17 May 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240107205\">Infection prevention and control and water, sanitation and hygiene in health facilities during Ebola or Marburg disease outbreaks: rapid assessment tool, user guide.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240107328\">Assessment and management of health and care workers with possible occupational exposures to Orthoebolavirus or Orthomarburgvirus: implementation guidance</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241515894\">Optimized Supportive Care for Ebola Virus Disease. Clinical management standard operating procedures. WHO. 2019</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240032729\">Framework and toolkit for infection prevention and control in outbreak preparedness, readiness and response at the national level</a></li><li><a href=\"https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/386711\">Diagnostic testing for Ebola disease and Marburg virus disease: interim guidance, 9 July 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/considerations-for-border-health-and-points-of-entry-for-filovirus-disease-outbreaks\">Considerations for border health and points of entry for filovirus disease outbreaks</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Vaccines</strong></p><ul paraid=\"315150135\" paraeid=\"{e2328c95-d2ef-46bc-aca5-28eefb81d988}{76}\"><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/B09865\">Third meeting of the WHO Technical Advisory Group on candidate vaccine prioritization (TAG-CVP) for Bundibugyo virus disease outbreak response: meeting report, 31 July 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/02-07-2026-patient-enrolment-begins-in-a-scientific-trial-to-identify-the-first-effective-treatments-for-bundibugyo-virus-disease\">WHO press release regarding a clinical trial to find effective treatments against Bundibugyo virus disease.</a>&nbsp;<br /></li></ul><p><strong>Training</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/26-03-2025-who-launches-online-training-to-strengthen-filovirus-outbreak-response\">WHO Launches Online Training to Strengthen Filovirus Outbreak Response</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Prior Bundibugyo virus disease events, DRC (2012)</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2012_09_14-en\">Disease Outbreak News. Ebola outbreak in Democratic Republic of Congo &ndash; update. WHO. 14 September 2012</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2012_10_26-en\">Disease Outbreak News. Ebola outbreak in Democratic Republic of Congo &ndash; update. WHO. 26 October 2012</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Background and reference</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ebola-disease\">Ebola disease fact sheet</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/ebola-vaccines\">Ebola vaccines</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Citable reference:</strong> World Health Organization (8 October 2026). Disease Outbreak News; Bundibugyo Virus Disease, Democratic Republic of the Congo. Available at: <a href=\"https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON619\">https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON619</a>&nbsp;<br /></p>","Summary":"On 5 October 2026, WHO was informed about a laboratory-confirmed imported case of Bundibugyo virus disease (BVD) in Kenya with travel history from the\u202fDemocratic\u202fRepublic of\u202fthe Congo. The patient travelled from the Democratic Republic of the Congo to Kenya through Uganda. The patient was hospitalized and treated in Kenya but subsequently died on 5 October. Public health response measures have been initiated in Kenya and Uganda, including contact tracing. \r\n\r\nSince the last Disease Outbreak News was published on 25 September 2026, the Bundibugyo virus outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo has expanded to one additional health zone,  Alimbongo health zone in North Kivu, adding to the geographic footprint of an outbreak that remains concentrated in the eastern part of the country. \r\n\r\nAs of 6 October 2026, the country has recorded 8728 confirmed cases and 4205 confirmed deaths, corresponding to a crude case fatality ratio (CFR) of 48.2%. The cases have been reported from 64 health zones across seven provinces out of 26 provinces of the country: Bas-U\u00e9l\u00e9, Haut-U\u00e9l\u00e9, Ituri, North Kivu, South Kivu, Sud Ubangi and Tshopo.  \r\n\r\nAt the national level, transmission remains intense, although the burden is unevenly distributed. In some areas, there are clear signs that the epidemic is slowing down, although in others it continues to grow. Over the past three completed epidemiological weeks, new weekly cases nationwide have increased by 2.5% from 475 to 487, while new weekly deaths remain stable at 224 compared to 222. \r\n\r\nThe persistently high mortality, together with the large number of deaths occurring outside treatment centres, continues to point to delays in identifying illness and reaching appropriate care. Since the last Disease Outbreak News, 264 of the 372 reported deaths occurred in communities and 108 in treatment centres. \r\n\r\nOn 1 October, the Netherlands reported the medical evacuation of a healthcare worker who tested positive by PCR for Bundibugyo virus (BDBV) in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. \u202fAt the time of reporting, the patient has been undergoing treatment in the Netherlands. ","PublicationDateAndTime":"2026-10-08T15:16:26Z","TitleSuffix":"","UseOverrideTitle":false,"Title":"Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo","Epidemiology":"<p><div><p paraid=\"710405995\" paraeid=\"{318e963b-1f55-4cb5-ae85-786c893acf77}{176}\">Bundibugyo virus disease (BVD) is a severe Ebola disease caused by the Bundibugyo virus, one of the <em>Orthoebolavirus species</em>. It is a zoonotic disease, with fruit bats suspected to be the natural reservoir.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"677574308\" paraeid=\"{318e963b-1f55-4cb5-ae85-786c893acf77}{190}\">Human infection is thought to initially occur through close contact with the blood or secretions of infected wildlife, such as bats or non-human primates, and it subsequently spreads from person-to-person through direct contact with the blood, secretions, organs, or other bodily fluids of infected individuals or contaminated surfaces and materials. Transmission is particularly amplified in health-care settings when infection prevention and control (IPC) measures are inadequate, and during unsafe burial practices involving direct contact with deceased individuals.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1295580800\" paraeid=\"{318e963b-1f55-4cb5-ae85-786c893acf77}{252}\">The incubation period for BVD ranges from two to 21 days, and infected individuals are not infectious until symptom onset. Early symptoms such as fever, fatigue, muscle pain, headache, and sore throat are non-specific, which complicates clinical diagnosis and can delay detection. These symptoms then progress to gastrointestinal symptoms, organ dysfunction, and, in some cases, haemorrhagic manifestations.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"2006760097\" paraeid=\"{bbfa34a9-75ed-45a4-974c-afadb44908e9}{55}\">The CFR from the previous two BVD outbreaks, reported in Uganda and in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in 2007 and 2012, were 30% and 50%, respectively.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"935143066\" paraeid=\"{bbfa34a9-75ed-45a4-974c-afadb44908e9}{103}\">Differentiating BVD from other endemic febrile illnesses such as malaria is challenging without laboratory confirmation. Outbreak control relies on rapid case identification and investigation, isolation and care, contact tracing, safe burials and strong community engagement, as no approved vaccines or specific treatments currently exist for BVD.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1482957527\" paraeid=\"{bbfa34a9-75ed-45a4-974c-afadb44908e9}{121}\">Since first detected in May 2026, this BVD outbreak has rapidly evolved into a large and geographically expanding epidemic in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, with sustained transmission, high mortality and an increasing risk of further international spread. The current outbreak is the second documented Bundibugyo virus disease outbreak in the country, after the 2012 outbreak, and the largest Ebola disease outbreak ever recorded in the Democratic Republic of the Congo irrespective of virus species. The population at greatest risk of exposure is concentrated in communities living in and moving through the health areas with active transmission.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div></p>","OverrideTitle":"","Advice":"<div><p paraid=\"1270548175\" paraeid=\"{1b66a25d-dc73-4b2c-a10e-a39ed8cbee4c}{217}\">Based on the currently available information, WHO advises against any restriction of travel to, or trade with, affected countries. WHO continues to closely monitor and, where necessary, verify travel and trade measures in relation to this event.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1471374517\" paraeid=\"{1b66a25d-dc73-4b2c-a10e-a39ed8cbee4c}{235}\">The updated <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Temporary Recommendations</a> issued to States Parties on 24 August 2026 underscore the importance of coordinated outbreak control, strengthened cross\u2011border collaboration, and sustained surveillance and preparedness to prevent further regional spread and ensure an effective public health response. Rapid recognition of cases, testing and optimized supportive care can reduce mortality, and improve community perceptions and acceptance of health care within the response.&nbsp;</p></div>","Assessment":"<div><div><p paraid=\"2105732305\" paraeid=\"{5637edb1-0f70-4e72-9deb-7a0d9cced83e}{80}\">On 14 August 2026, WHO reassessed the risk of the outbreak of BVD, incorporating newly available information on the evolving situation. The risk for countries sharing land borders with the Democratic Republic of the Congo was separated from the risk for other countries in the African Region.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1383071648\" paraeid=\"{5637edb1-0f70-4e72-9deb-7a0d9cced83e}{126}\">The risk in the Democratic Republic of the Congo was assessed as very high, the risk for countries sharing land borders with the Democratic Republic of the Congo was assessed as high, and the risks for the rest of the African region and at the global level was again assessed as low.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1761089879\" paraeid=\"{5637edb1-0f70-4e72-9deb-7a0d9cced83e}{180}\">A detailed rapid risk assessment is currently ongoing to assess the risk in Kenya and will be reported once available.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"788217968\" paraeid=\"{5637edb1-0f70-4e72-9deb-7a0d9cced83e}{188}\">For further information, please see <a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment-ebola-disease-caused-by-bundibugyo-virus--democratic-republic-of-the-congo-v4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment-Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo v4</a>.</p></div><strong></strong></div>","Overview":"<div><p paraid=\"1555743484\" paraeid=\"{daed1eef-c9a0-4e82-8a92-d151522b4909}{143}\">Since the previous <a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON618\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Disease Outbreak News</a> was published on 25 September 2026, an additional 775 confirmed cases, including 379 confirmed deaths, have been reported in the Democratic Republic of the Congo.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"974233148\" paraeid=\"{daed1eef-c9a0-4e82-8a92-d151522b4909}{223}\">In the most recent 24-hour period, as of 6 October, 62 new confirmed cases were reported, with Ituri accounting for 33 cases and North Kivu for 30. No new cases were reported in Bas-U&eacute;l&eacute;, South Kivu or Sud-Ubangi during that period. Across the preceding seven days, the average number of new cases rose to 72 per day.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1357919205\" paraeid=\"{8686e834-c6e6-4be7-9ad7-766357d4700a}{4}\">As of 6 October, cumulative confirmed cases have reached 8728, including 4205 deaths and 2269 recoveries. The overall crude CFR is 48.2%.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1158755919\" paraeid=\"{8686e834-c6e6-4be7-9ad7-766357d4700a}{44}\">Confirmed cases have now been identified in 64 health zones across seven provinces. Forty-eight of the 64 affected zones reported at least one new case during the preceding 21 days, while 16 reported no recent cases. Ituri remains the most extensively affected province, with 28 of 36 health zones reporting cases during this outbreak, followed by North Kivu (17/34), Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; (7/13), Tshopo (7/23), Bas-U&eacute;l&eacute; (3/11), South Kivu (1/34) and Sud-Ubangi (1/16). Alimbongo in North Kivu is the most recently affected health zone and reported four confirmed cases, including two deaths.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"746795431\" paraeid=\"{8686e834-c6e6-4be7-9ad7-766357d4700a}{64}\"><strong>Figure 1. Distribution of cumulative confirmed cases of Bundibugyo virus disease in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, as of 6 October 2026</strong></p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/don_evd_drc_map_20261008b7527085-ad42-4993-a689-3e367b6d341f.png?sfvrsn=d0ebda9f_2\" height=\"527\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"DON_EVD_DRC_map_20261008\" width=\"759\" alt=\"EVD DRC map\" sf-size=\"253151\" /><p paraid=\"746795431\" paraeid=\"{8686e834-c6e6-4be7-9ad7-766357d4700a}{64}\"><strong></strong><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">Ituri remains the epicentre, with 6480 cumulative confirmed cases and 2989 deaths. North Kivu follows with 1755 confirmed cases and 1013 deaths, and continues to record the highest provincial CFR, at 57.7%. Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; has reported 364 cases and 153 deaths, while Tshopo has recorded 51 cases and 17 deaths. The less-affected provinces continue to report substantially fewer cases, but transmission persists in several of them. The most affected health zones were Beni and Katwa in North Kivu, and Rwampara, Bunia and Mandima in Ituri. The continued occurrence of cases across multiple provinces shows that the outbreak remains geographically active even as intensity varies between health zones.&nbsp;</span></p></div><p><div><p paraid=\"1231484003\" paraeid=\"{8686e834-c6e6-4be7-9ad7-766357d4700a}{185}\">Contact follow-up remains a major operational pressure and has fluctuated below the response target in recent weeks. Coverage was 87.6% on 16 September, briefly above the target of 85%, but fell to 74.7% by 25 September. Although coverage subsequently improved to 82.0% on 27 September, it remained below target and stood at 80.4% in the latest reporting period, with 23 741 of 29 535 contacts reached as of 4 October. The current level therefore reflects a persistent gap in the ability to monitor exposed people consistently throughout the 21-day follow-up period.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"855615797\" paraeid=\"{8686e834-c6e6-4be7-9ad7-766357d4700a}{203}\"><strong>Figure 2: Number of confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, by date of notification, as of 6 October 2026&nbsp;</strong></p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/don_evd_drc_cases_20261008a8cf73c1-2c8f-400d-aca1-afd38b2092d5.png?sfvrsn=caf15ec0_2\" height=\"555\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"DON_EVD_DRC_cases_20261008\" width=\"823\" alt=\"EVD DRC\" sf-size=\"48683\" /></div><p><strong>Figure 3: Number of deaths among confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in the Democratic Republic of the Congo by date of notification, as of 6 October 2026.&nbsp;</strong></p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/don_evd_drc_deaths_2026100882e14d21-90ae-4bca-b0fd-d8a4aaa024dc.png?sfvrsn=1ee51d56_2\" height=\"567\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"DON_EVD_DRC_deaths_20261008\" width=\"815\" alt=\"EVD DRC\" sf-size=\"41871\" /><strong style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">Kenya&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong><br /><div><p paraid=\"1405241295\" paraeid=\"{318e963b-1f55-4cb5-ae85-786c893acf77}{24}\">On 6 October 2026, the Ministry of Health of Kenya reported the first laboratory-confirmed imported case of BVD in Kenya.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1352828716\" paraeid=\"{318e963b-1f55-4cb5-ae85-786c893acf77}{30}\">According to an official statement from the Ministry of Health, the individual is an adult who lived in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. The patient became ill approximately one month ago and was treated in several hospitals while in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. The patient travelled from the Democratic Republic of the Congo to Kampala, Uganda, by road before arriving in Nairobi, Kenya by air on 3 October. It is currently unclear if and when he was symptomatic during these travels, but upon arriving ill in Kenya, the patient was transported by a relative directly to Nairobi Hospital and isolated in the facility. A sample collected from the patient on 5 October was positive for BDBV at both the National Virology Reference Laboratory and the Kenya Medical Research Institute Laboratory on the same day. The patient was given supportive treatment but subsequently died.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"2115137207\" paraeid=\"{318e963b-1f55-4cb5-ae85-786c893acf77}{76}\">A safe and dignified burial has been conducted. Public health response actions were immediately initiated. Contact tracing is ongoing, including for the international flight.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"56691084\" paraeid=\"{318e963b-1f55-4cb5-ae85-786c893acf77}{90}\">Given the timelines of the illness, and the one month time from initial symptoms to death, it is presumed he was not initially sick with BVD, which does not usually have such a long progression from infection to symptoms and death, but became infected with BVD in a health facility or community while seeking care for another ailment.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"379448494\" paraeid=\"{318e963b-1f55-4cb5-ae85-786c893acf77}{138}\"><strong>The Netherlands&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong></p></div><div><p paraid=\"35021750\" paraeid=\"{318e963b-1f55-4cb5-ae85-786c893acf77}{144}\">On 1 October 2026, the Netherlands reported the medical evacuation of a healthcare worker who tested positive by PCR for BDBV in the Democratic Republic of the Congo where the patient was working on the Ebola disease outbreak response. Following confirmation of the diagnosis, the patient was safely repatriated to the Netherlands under strict medical and infection-prevention procedures, in close coordination with the relevant health authorities in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and the Netherlands. As with previous medically evacuated cases, this case will not be reported as a local case of BVD in the Netherlands.&nbsp;</p></div></p>","DonId":"2026-DON619","Provider":"dynamicProvider372"},{"Id":"5df1872c-3aab-4e1b-9d78-475f6ead4040","LastModified":"2026-10-22T08:00:35Z","PublicationDate":"2026-09-25T15:30:18Z","DateCreated":"2026-09-25T15:30:19Z","IncludeInSitemap":true,"SystemSourceKey":null,"UrlName":"2026-DON621","ItemDefaultUrl":"/2026-DON621","Response":"<p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">For detailed information about the ongoing public health response actions by the Ministry of Health, WHO and partners please refer to the latest situation reports published by the WHO Regional Office for Africa: </span><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" style=\"text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;white-space:inherit;\">Ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></p><div><p paraid=\"2108908504\" paraeid=\"{1b66a25d-dc73-4b2c-a10e-a39ed8cbee4c}{7}\">Health authorities in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in collaboration with WHO and partners, are continuing to implement and coordinate extensive public health measures, including disease surveillance, laboratory testing, infection prevention and control, clinical care, community engagement, research, logistics and support for response interventions and essential health services, engaging donors and mobilizing additional resources to address critical funding gaps and sustain response operations across affected and at-risk areas. A substantial scale-up is ongoing across all response pillars to get ahead of the outbreak.&nbsp;</p></div>","FurtherInformation":"<p><strong>Regular Information products on the outbreak o</strong><strong>f Bundibugyo virus disease </strong><strong></strong></p><ul><li><strong>Daily update: </strong><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/alert-and-response\">Epidemiological update on BVD outbreak in Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li><li><strong>Published weekly: </strong><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a> | WHO| Regional Office for Africa </li><li><strong>Published biweekly:&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news\"><strong></strong>Disease Outbreak News | All Hazards Public Health Events, Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li></ul><p><strong>Additional information</strong></p><p><strong style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">Current outbreak: declarations and status</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/05-06-2026-africa-cdc-and-who-launch-joint-continental-ebola-response-plan\">Africa CDC and WHO launch joint continental Ebola response plan</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/publications/bundibugyo-ebola-virus-continental-preparedness-and-response-plan-june-november-2026\">Bundibugyo Ebola virus | Continental preparedness and response plan: June-November 2026</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern\">Epidemic of Ebola Disease caused by Bundibugyo virus in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda determined a public health emergency of international concern</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://administration.sante.gouv.cd/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Declaration-de-la-17e-Epidemie-de-la-maladie-a-virus-Ebola-dans-les-zones-de-sante-de-Rwampara-Mongwalu-et-Bunia-dans-la-province-dIturi.pdf\">The Ministry of Public Health, Hygiene and Social Welfare, DRC, officially declares the 17th Ebola Disease outbreak</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/democratic-republic-congo-confirms-new-ebola-outbreak-who-scales-upsupport\">WHO Democratic Republic of Congo confirms new Ebola outbreak</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/28-05-2026-message-by-the-who-director-general-to-the-people-of-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo\">Message by the WHO Director-General to the people of the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/frontline-trust-day-julienne-anoko-whos-ebola-community-engagement-officer-drc\">At the frontline of trust: a day with Julienne Anoko WHO's Ebola Community Engagement Officer in DRC</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/decentralized-testing-speeds-ebola-response-democratic-republic-congo\">Decentralized testing speeds up Ebola response in the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/uganda/news/uganda-ends-ebola-outbreak-following-completion-42-day-countdown\">Uganda ends Ebola outbreak following completion of 42-day countdown | WHO | Regional Office for Afri&hellip;</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\">Second meeting of the IHR Emergency Committee on the epidemic of Ebola Bundibugyo virus disease in the Democratic Republic of the Congo &ndash; Temporary recommendations</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>WHO Rapid risk assessments</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment-ebola-disease-caused-by-bundibugyo-virus--democratic-republic-of-the-congo-v4\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment-Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo v4</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Epidemiological updates and situation reports</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Weekly External Situation Report. EBOLA BUNDIBUGYO VIRUS DISEASE OUTBREAK Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/ebola/situation-summary/index.html\">Ebola Outbreak: Current Situation | Ebola | CDC</a></li><li><a href=\"https://insp.cd/category/sitrep/\">Daily situation report, Ministry of Health, Democratic Republic of the Congo.</a></li></ul><p><strong>Published Disease Outbreak News (current outbreak)</strong><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\"></span></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON614\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 1 August 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON615\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 14 August 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON616\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 28 August 2026</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Clinical management, IPC, and occupational safety</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/B09774\">WHO guidelines for the clinical management of filovirus disease</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240111332\">Infection prevention and control guideline for Ebola and Marburg disease. WHO.17 May 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240107205\">Infection prevention and control and water, sanitation and hygiene in health facilities during Ebola or Marburg disease outbreaks: rapid assessment tool, user guide.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240107328\">Assessment and management of health and care workers with possible occupational exposures to Orthoebolavirus or Orthomarburgvirus: implementation guidance</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241515894\">Optimized Supportive Care for Ebola Virus Disease. Clinical management standard operating procedures. WHO. 2019</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240032729\">Framework and toolkit for infection prevention and control in outbreak preparedness, readiness and response at the national level</a></li><li><a href=\"https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/386711\">Diagnostic testing for Ebola disease and Marburg virus disease: interim guidance, 9 July 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/considerations-for-border-health-and-points-of-entry-for-filovirus-disease-outbreaks\">Considerations for border health and points of entry for filovirus disease outbreaks</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Vaccines</strong></p><ul paraid=\"315150135\" paraeid=\"{e2328c95-d2ef-46bc-aca5-28eefb81d988}{76}\"><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/B09865\">Third meeting of the WHO Technical Advisory Group on candidate vaccine prioritization (TAG-CVP) for Bundibugyo virus disease outbreak response: meeting report, 31 July 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/02-07-2026-patient-enrolment-begins-in-a-scientific-trial-to-identify-the-first-effective-treatments-for-bundibugyo-virus-disease\">WHO press release regarding a clinical trial to find effective treatments against Bundibugyo virus disease.</a>&nbsp;<br /></li></ul><p><strong>Training</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/26-03-2025-who-launches-online-training-to-strengthen-filovirus-outbreak-response\">WHO Launches Online Training to Strengthen Filovirus Outbreak Response</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Prior Bundibugyo virus disease events, DRC (2012)</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2012_09_14-en\">Disease Outbreak News. Ebola outbreak in Democratic Republic of Congo &ndash; update. WHO. 14 September 2012</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2012_10_26-en\">Disease Outbreak News. Ebola outbreak in Democratic Republic of Congo &ndash; update. WHO. 26 October 2012</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Background and reference</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ebola-disease\">Ebola disease fact sheet</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/ebola-vaccines\">Ebola vaccines</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Citable reference:</strong> World Health Organization (25 September 2026). Disease Outbreak News; Bundibugyo Virus Disease, Democratic Republic of the Congo. Available at: <a href=\"https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON621\">https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON621</a> <br /></p>","Summary":"Since the last Disease Outbreak News was published on 11 September 2026, the Bundibugyo virus outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo has expanded further, with two additional health zones affected. These include Bulu health zone in a new province, Sud Ubangi, located on the north-west part of the country and Dungu health zone in Haut-U\u00e9l\u00e9 province, bordering South Sudan. This brings the total number of affected health zones to 63 across seven provinces out of 26 provinces of the country: Bas-U\u00e9l\u00e9, Haut-U\u00e9l\u00e9, Ituri, North Kivu, South Kivu, Sud Ubangi and Tshopo. This latest geographic expansion increases the risk of cross-border transmission. \r\n\r\nAs of 23 September 2026, the Democratic Republic of the Congo has reported 7890 confirmed cases, including 3799 deaths, resulting in a crude case fatality ratio (CFR) of 48.1%.  \r\n\r\nAt the national level, the number of new cases reported each day remains high. However, the situation varies across the country, with some provinces and health zones experiencing much higher levels of transmission than others.  \r\n\r\nThe continuously high CFR, and especially the continuous high rate of deaths occurring in communities, highlights the seriousness of the disease and the persistent challenges in timely case detection and access to early and adequate patient care. These delays can contribute to preventable illness and deaths among people in affected and newly affected areas, while also allowing transmission to continue within households, communities, and healthcare settings. ","PublicationDateAndTime":"2026-09-25T15:16:26Z","TitleSuffix":"","UseOverrideTitle":false,"Title":"Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo","Epidemiology":"<div><p paraid=\"710405995\" paraeid=\"{3d7d4911-5037-4b57-bf25-d27f8480f4ed}{12}\">Bundibugyo virus disease (BVD) is a severe Ebola disease caused by the Bundibugyo virus, one of the <em>Orthoebolavirus </em>species. It is a zoonotic disease, with fruit bats suspected to be the natural reservoir.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"677574308\" paraeid=\"{3d7d4911-5037-4b57-bf25-d27f8480f4ed}{26}\">Human infection is thought to occur through close contact with the blood or secretions of infected wildlife, such as bats or non-human primates, and it subsequently spreads from person-to-person through direct contact with the blood, secretions, organs, or other bodily fluids of infected individuals or contaminated surfaces and materials. Transmission is particularly amplified in health-care settings when IPC measures are inadequate and during unsafe burial practices involving direct contact with deceased individuals.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1295580800\" paraeid=\"{3d7d4911-5037-4b57-bf25-d27f8480f4ed}{78}\">The incubation period for BVD ranges from two to 21 days, and infected individuals are not infectious until symptom onset. Early symptoms such as fever, fatigue, muscle pain, headache, and sore throat are non-specific, which complicates clinical diagnosis and can delay detection. These symptoms then progress to gastrointestinal symptoms, organ dysfunction, and, in some cases, haemorrhagic manifestations.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"2006760097\" paraeid=\"{3d7d4911-5037-4b57-bf25-d27f8480f4ed}{136}\">The historical CFR from the past two BVD outbreaks, reported in Uganda and in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in 2007 and 2012, were 30% and 50%, respectively.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"935143066\" paraeid=\"{3d7d4911-5037-4b57-bf25-d27f8480f4ed}{186}\">Differentiating BVD from other endemic febrile illnesses such as malaria is challenging without laboratory confirmation using polymerase chain reaction (PCR) or antigen- or antibody-based assays. Outbreak control relies on rapid case identification, isolation and care, contact tracing, safe burials and strong community engagement, as no approved vaccines or specific treatments currently exist for BVD.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"344893295\" paraeid=\"{3d7d4911-5037-4b57-bf25-d27f8480f4ed}{214}\">Since first detected in May 2026, this BVD outbreak has rapidly evolved into a large and geographically expanding epidemic in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, with sustained transmission, high mortality and an increasing risk of further international spread. The current outbreak is the second documented Bundibugyo virus disease outbreak in the country, after the 2012 outbreak, and the largest Ebola disease outbreak ever recorded in the Democratic Republic of the Congo irrespective of Ebola virus species. The population at greatest risk of exposure is concentrated in communities living in and moving through the health areas with active transmission.&nbsp;</p></div>","OverrideTitle":"","Advice":"<div><p paraid=\"1270548175\" paraeid=\"{1b66a25d-dc73-4b2c-a10e-a39ed8cbee4c}{217}\">Based on the currently available information, WHO advises against any restriction of travel to, or trade with, affected countries. WHO continues to closely monitor and, where necessary, verify travel and trade measures in relation to this event.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1471374517\" paraeid=\"{1b66a25d-dc73-4b2c-a10e-a39ed8cbee4c}{235}\">The updated <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Temporary Recommendations</a> issued to States Parties on 24 August 2026 underscore the importance of coordinated outbreak control, strengthened cross\u2011border collaboration, and sustained surveillance and preparedness to prevent further regional spread and ensure an effective public health response. Rapid recognition of cases, testing and optimized supportive care can reduce mortality, and improve community perceptions and acceptance of health care within the response.&nbsp;</p></div>","Assessment":"<div><p paraid=\"2105732305\" paraeid=\"{1b66a25d-dc73-4b2c-a10e-a39ed8cbee4c}{91}\">On 14 August 2026, WHO reassessed the risk of the outbreak of BVD, incorporating newly available information on the evolving situation. The risk for countries sharing land borders with the Democratic Republic of the Congo was separated from the risk for other countries in the African Region.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1383071648\" paraeid=\"{1b66a25d-dc73-4b2c-a10e-a39ed8cbee4c}{137}\">The risk in the Democratic Republic of the Congo was assessed as very high, the risk for countries sharing land borders with the Democratic Republic of the Congo was assessed as high, and the risks for the rest of the African region and at the global level was again assessed as low.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"788217968\" paraeid=\"{1b66a25d-dc73-4b2c-a10e-a39ed8cbee4c}{193}\">For further information, please see <a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment-ebola-disease-caused-by-bundibugyo-virus--democratic-republic-of-the-congo-v4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment-Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo v4</a>&nbsp;</p><strong></strong></div>","Overview":"<p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">Since the previous </span><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON617\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" style=\"text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;white-space:inherit;\">Disease Outbreak News</a><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\"> was published on 11 September 2026, an additional 1133 confirmed cases, including 532 confirmed deaths, have been reported in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. The seven-day moving average shows a resurgence in early September followed by a decline over the most recent reporting days. However, the aggregate national trend conceals substantial variation in transmission intensity across affected provinces and health zones.&nbsp;</span></p><div><p paraid=\"1173713190\" paraeid=\"{c42fc159-e721-47a9-8cf9-623c6bdc72cd}{162}\">As of 23 September, the Democratic Republic of the Congo has reported a total of 7890 confirmed cases, including 3799 deaths (CFR 48.1%). A total of 1966 patients have recovered to date.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1555743484\" paraeid=\"{c42fc159-e721-47a9-8cf9-623c6bdc72cd}{244}\">Confirmed cases have been reported from 63 health zones across seven provinces, with 48 health zones from six provinces reporting at least one case in the last 21 days. Ituri remains the most affected province, with 28 of its 36 health zones reporting cases, followed by North Kivu (16/34), Tshopo (7/23), Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; (7/13), Bas-U&eacute;l&eacute; (3/11), South Kivu (1/34), and Sud Ubangi (1/16). No new cases have been reported from South Kivu province since 29 May 2026.&nbsp;&nbsp;Dungu Health Zone in Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; province and Bulu in Sud Ubangi are the most recently affected areas. As of 23 September, 70 new confirmed cases had been reported in the preceding 24 hours from 26 health zones located in Ituri, North Kivu, Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute;, Bas U&eacute;l&eacute; and Tshopo provinces.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"746795431\" paraeid=\"{5dc43249-f5f0-4cd9-b438-8381718e268b}{165}\"><strong>Figure 1. Distribution of cumulative confirmed cases of Bundibugyo virus disease in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, as of 23 September</strong></p><p paraid=\"746795431\" paraeid=\"{5dc43249-f5f0-4cd9-b438-8381718e268b}{165}\"><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/20260925_don_evd_drc_mapd51d9a14-4ce8-482d-8159-3c261f8f7607.png?sfvrsn=53eac9ca_2\" height=\"530\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"20260925_DON_EVD_DRC_map\" width=\"759\" alt=\"DRC map\" sf-size=\"251272\" /></p></div><p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">Ituri continues to be the epicentre of the outbreak, accounting for 6032 confirmed cases since the start of the outbreak, including 868 new confirmed cases reported in the previous 21 days, as of 23 September. North Kivu is the second most affected province, with a cumulative number of 1480 confirmed cases, including 567 reported in the last 21 days, as of 23 September. North Kivu province continues to report the highest CFR (59.7%) observed in this outbreak; and investigations are ongoing to better understand the factors contributing to this elevated mortality rate.&nbsp;</span></p><div><p paraid=\"1831920683\" paraeid=\"{4350f06f-00bd-4012-afc0-2ae31451c1bd}{63}\">In Ituri, case incidence continues to decline gradually from the peak observed in mid-August, although transmission remains at elevated levels. North Kivu, in contrast, has experienced a substantial increase in incidence, reaching its highest reported level in mid-September, followed by a decline in recent reporting days. Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; continues to demonstrate sustained transmission, albeit at levels below the peak recorded in late August, while Tshopo is showing renewed transmission activity following a period of low incidence. In Bas-U&eacute;l&eacute;, transmission remains sporadic, whereas no recent evidence of transmission has been reported in Sud-Kivu. Sud Ubangi is the seventh province to report a confirmed case of BVD, with one case that was reported on 10 September (Figure 2).&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"486817034\" paraeid=\"{4350f06f-00bd-4012-afc0-2ae31451c1bd}{69}\">The number of individuals requiring follow-up as contact has also risen considerably with the expansion of the outbreak. As of 23 September, 83.4% of identified contacts were successfully monitored during the previous 24 hours with 26 980 contacts seen out of 32 342 requiring follow up. The large volume of contacts under surveillance highlights the extent of potential exposure within affected communities and the substantial demands placed on response operations.&nbsp;</p><div><p paraid=\"1647073309\" paraeid=\"{4350f06f-00bd-4012-afc0-2ae31451c1bd}{161}\">The response is being implemented in a challenging humanitarian environment, where conflict, insecurity, displacement, and limited access to basic services continue to affect outbreak control.&nbsp;&nbsp;These constraints continue to hamper surveillance, case finding, contact tracing, infection prevention and control, and timely access to appropriate care, thereby limiting the overall effectiveness of response activities.&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"855615797\" paraeid=\"{4350f06f-00bd-4012-afc0-2ae31451c1bd}{167}\"><strong>Figure 2: Number of confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, by date of notification, as of 23 September 2026</strong></p><p paraid=\"855615797\" paraeid=\"{4350f06f-00bd-4012-afc0-2ae31451c1bd}{167}\"><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/don_evd_drc_cases_202609258efbc07a-a8da-404c-988d-66220ce15270.png?sfvrsn=20b289fa_2\" height=\"561\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"DON_EVD_DRC_cases_20260925\" width=\"816\" alt=\"DRC map\" sf-size=\"50289\" /></p></div><div><p paraid=\"1268974260\" paraeid=\"{4350f06f-00bd-4012-afc0-2ae31451c1bd}{212}\"><strong>Figure 3: Number of deaths among confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in the Democratic Republic of the Congo by date of notification, as of 23 September 2026.&nbsp;</strong></p></div></div><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/don_evd_drc_deaths_202609256fa0eafb-28aa-4034-9fe0-805f476d9985.png?sfvrsn=2239f0ee_2\" height=\"562\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"DON_EVD_DRC_deaths_20260925\" width=\"818\" alt=\"EVD deaths\" sf-size=\"44707\" /><br /></p>","DonId":"2026-DON621","Provider":"dynamicProvider372"},{"Id":"0381aabe-6109-425c-a3f9-2b33cb482486","LastModified":"2026-09-10T08:52:27Z","PublicationDate":"2026-09-10T08:52:02Z","DateCreated":"2026-09-10T08:52:27Z","IncludeInSitemap":true,"SystemSourceKey":null,"UrlName":"2026-DON617","ItemDefaultUrl":"/2026-DON617","Response":"<p>For detailed information about the ongoing public health response actions by the Ministry of Health, WHO and partners please refer to the latest situation reports published by the WHO Regional Office for Africa: <a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a> </p><p>Health authorities in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in collaboration with WHO and partners, are continuing to implement and coordinate extensive public health measures, including disease surveillance, laboratory testing, infection prevention and control, clinical care, community engagement, research, logistics and support for response interventions and essential health services, engaging donors and mobilizing additional resources to address critical funding gaps and sustain response operations across affected and at-risk areas. A substantial scale-up is ongoing across all response pillars to get ahead of the outbreak.&nbsp;</p>","FurtherInformation":"<p><strong>Regular Information products on the outbreak o</strong><strong>f Bundibugyo virus disease </strong><strong></strong></p><ul><li><strong>Daily update: </strong><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/alert-and-response\">Epidemiological update on BVD outbreak in Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li><li><strong>Published weekly: </strong><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a> | WHO| Regional Office for Africa </li><li><strong>Published biweekly:</strong><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news\"><strong></strong>Disease Outbreak News | All Hazards Public Health Events, Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li></ul><p><strong>Additional information</strong></p><p><strong style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">Current outbreak: declarations and status</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/05-06-2026-africa-cdc-and-who-launch-joint-continental-ebola-response-plan\">Africa CDC and WHO launch joint continental Ebola response plan</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/publications/bundibugyo-ebola-virus-continental-preparedness-and-response-plan-june-november-2026\">Bundibugyo Ebola virus | Continental preparedness and response plan: June-November 2026</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern\">Epidemic of Ebola Disease caused by Bundibugyo virus in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda determined a public health emergency of international concern</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://administration.sante.gouv.cd/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Declaration-de-la-17e-Epidemie-de-la-maladie-a-virus-Ebola-dans-les-zones-de-sante-de-Rwampara-Mongwalu-et-Bunia-dans-la-province-dIturi.pdf\">The Ministry of Public Health, Hygiene and Social Welfare, DRC, officially declares the 17th Ebola Disease outbreak</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/democratic-republic-congo-confirms-new-ebola-outbreak-who-scales-upsupport\">WHO Democratic Republic of Congo confirms new Ebola outbreak</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/28-05-2026-message-by-the-who-director-general-to-the-people-of-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo\">Message by the WHO Director-General to the people of the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/frontline-trust-day-julienne-anoko-whos-ebola-community-engagement-officer-drc\">At the frontline of trust: a day with Julienne Anoko WHO's Ebola Community Engagement Officer in DRC</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/decentralized-testing-speeds-ebola-response-democratic-republic-congo\">Decentralized testing speeds up Ebola response in the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/uganda/news/uganda-ends-ebola-outbreak-following-completion-42-day-countdown\">Uganda ends Ebola outbreak following completion of 42-day countdown | WHO | Regional Office for Afri&hellip;</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\">Second meeting of the IHR Emergency Committee on the epidemic of Ebola Bundibugyo virus disease in the Democratic Republic of the Congo &ndash; Temporary recommendations</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>WHO Rapid risk assessments</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment-ebola-disease-caused-by-bundibugyo-virus--democratic-republic-of-the-congo-v4\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment-Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo v4</a><span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\"></span><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Epidemiological updates and situation reports</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\">Weekly External Situation Report. EBOLA BUNDIBUGYO VIRUS DISEASE OUTBREAK Democratic Republic of the Congo | Uganda.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/ebola/situation-summary/index.html\">Ebola Outbreak: Current Situation | Ebola | CDC</a></li><li><a href=\"https://insp.cd/category/sitrep/\">Daily situation report, Ministry of Health, Democratic Republic of the Congo.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://health.go.ug/press-statements/\">Press Statements - Ministry of Health - Uganda</a></li><li><a href=\"https://evd-daily.health.go.ug/\">Ebola updates, Ministry of Health, Uganda</a> <br /></li></ul><p><strong>Published Disease Outbreak News (current outbreak)</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON602\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda 16 May 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON603\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo. 21 May 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON605\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo. 29 May 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON606\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda 8 June 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON607\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda 13 June 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON608\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda 19 June 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON612\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda 3 July 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON613\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda 17 July 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON614\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 1 August 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON615\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 14 August 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON616\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo 28 August 2026</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Clinical management, IPC, and occupational safety</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/B09774\">WHO guidelines for the clinical management of filovirus disease</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240111332\">Infection prevention and control guideline for Ebola and Marburg disease. WHO.17 May 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240107205\">Infection prevention and control and water, sanitation and hygiene in health facilities during Ebola or Marburg disease outbreaks: rapid assessment tool, user guide.</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240107328\">Assessment and management of health and care workers with possible occupational exposures to Orthoebolavirus or Orthomarburgvirus: implementation guidance</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241515894\">Optimized Supportive Care for Ebola Virus Disease. Clinical management standard operating procedures. WHO. 2019</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240032729\">Framework and toolkit for infection prevention and control in outbreak preparedness, readiness and response at the national level</a></li><li><a href=\"https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/386711\">Diagnostic testing for Ebola disease and Marburg virus disease: interim guidance, 9 July 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/considerations-for-border-health-and-points-of-entry-for-filovirus-disease-outbreaks\">Considerations for border health and points of entry for filovirus disease outbreaks</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Vaccines</strong></p><ul paraid=\"315150135\" paraeid=\"{e2328c95-d2ef-46bc-aca5-28eefb81d988}{76}\"><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/B09865\">Third meeting of the WHO Technical Advisory Group on candidate vaccine prioritization (TAG-CVP) for Bundibugyo virus disease outbreak response: meeting report, 31 July 2026</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/02-07-2026-patient-enrolment-begins-in-a-scientific-trial-to-identify-the-first-effective-treatments-for-bundibugyo-virus-disease\">WHO press release regarding a clinical trial to find effective treatments against Bundibugyo virus disease.</a>&nbsp;<br /></li></ul><p><strong>Training</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/26-03-2025-who-launches-online-training-to-strengthen-filovirus-outbreak-response\">WHO Launches Online Training to Strengthen Filovirus Outbreak Response</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Prior Bundibugyo virus disease events, DRC (2012)</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2012_09_14-en\">Disease Outbreak News. Ebola outbreak in Democratic Republic of Congo &ndash; update. WHO. 14 September 2012</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2012_10_26-en\">Disease Outbreak News. Ebola outbreak in Democratic Republic of Congo &ndash; update. WHO. 26 October 2012</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Background and reference</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ebola-disease\">Ebola disease fact sheet</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/ebola-vaccines\">Ebola vaccines</a><br /></li></ul><p><strong>Citable reference:</strong> World Health Organization (10 September 2026). Disease Outbreak News; Bundibugyo Virus Disease, Democratic Republic of the Congo. Available at: <a href=\"https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON617\">https://www/who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak.news.item/2026-DON617</a><br /></p>","Summary":"Since the last Disease Outbreak News was published on 28 August 2026, the Bundibugyo virus outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo has expanded to one additional health zone, Kayna, in North Kivu. This increase brings the total number of affected health zones to 61 across six out of 26 provinces of the country: Bas-U\u00e9l\u00e9, Haut-U\u00e9l\u00e9, Ituri, North Kivu, South Kivu, and Tshopo.\r\nAs of 7 September 2026, the Democratic Republic of the Congo has reported 6757 confirmed cases, including 3267 deaths, resulting in a crude case fatality ratio (CFR) of 48.3%. The transmission dynamics remain variable across affected locations, with evidence of ongoing geographical expansion and sustained increase in cases in some health zones. The continuously high CFR highlights the seriousness of the disease and persistent challenges in timely case detection, access to early and adequate patient care, and effective interruption of transmission. Delayed detection of cases continues to increase the risk of further spread within households, communities, and healthcare settings.","PublicationDateAndTime":"2026-09-10T08:16:08Z","TitleSuffix":"","UseOverrideTitle":false,"Title":"Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo","Epidemiology":"<p>Bundibugyo virus disease (BVD) is a severe Ebola disease caused by the Bundibugyo virus, one of the <em>Orthoebolavirus </em>species. It is a zoonotic disease, with fruit bats suspected to be the natural reservoir.</p><p>Human infection is thought to occur through close contact with the blood or secretions of infected wildlife, such as bats or non-human primates, and it subsequently spreads from person-to-person through direct contact with the blood, secretions, organs, or other bodily fluids of infected individuals or contaminated surfaces and materials. Transmission is particularly amplified in health-care settings when IPC measures are inadequate and during unsafe burial practices involving direct contact with deceased individuals.</p><p>The incubation period for BVD ranges from two to 21 days, and infected individuals are not infectious until symptom onset. Early symptoms such as fever, fatigue, muscle pain, headache, and sore throat are non-specific, which complicates clinical diagnosis and can delay detection. These symptoms then progress to gastrointestinal symptoms, organ dysfunction, and, in some cases, haemorrhagic manifestations.</p><p>The historical CFR from the past two BVD outbreaks, reported in Uganda and in the Democratic Republic of the Congo in 2007 and 2012, were 30% and 50%, respectively.</p><p>Differentiating BVD from other endemic febrile illnesses such as malaria is challenging without laboratory confirmation using polymerase chain reaction (PCR) or antigen- or antibody-based assays. Outbreak control relies on rapid case identification, isolation and care, contact tracing, safe burials and strong community engagement, as no approved vaccines or specific treatments currently exist for BVD.</p><p>Since first detected in May 2026, this BVD outbreak has rapidly evolved into a large and geographically expanding epidemic in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, with sustained transmission, high mortality and an increasing risk of further international spread. The current outbreak is the second documented Bundibugyo virus disease outbreak in the country, after the 2012 outbreak, and the largest Ebola disease outbreak ever recorded in the Democratic Republic of the Congo irrespective of Ebola virus species. The population at greatest risk of exposure is concentrated in communities living in and moving through the health areas with active transmission.&nbsp;</p>","OverrideTitle":"","Advice":"<p>Based on the currently available information, WHO advises against any restriction of travel to, or trade with, affected countries. WHO continues to closely monitor and, where necessary, verify travel and trade measures in relation to this event.</p><p>The updated <a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\">Temporary Recommendations</a> issued to States Parties on 24 August 2026 underscore the importance of coordinated outbreak control, strengthened cross\u2011border collaboration, and sustained surveillance and preparedness to prevent further regional spread and ensure an effective public health response. Rapid recognition of cases, testing and optimized supportive care can reduce mortality, and improve community perceptions and acceptance of health care within the response.</p><p>On 7 August, the WHO Technical Advisory Group on candidate vaccine prioritization released a report regarding possible candidate vaccines for Bundibugyo virus disease. The members recommended that Ervebo, the only licensed Ebola vaccine (previously known as ebolavirus Zaire), be prioritized for inclusion in a randomized clinical trial in the context of the ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Following a review of additional evidence on 19 August 2026, the WHO Strategic Advisory Group of Experts on Immunization (SAGE) concluded that available evidence remains insufficient to support the programmatic use of Ervebo for the prevention of BVD, and that its efficacy against BVD in humans remains unknown. WHO therefore recommends that Ervebo be used for BVD only within the context of a research protocol. &nbsp;Ervebo vaccination of healthcare and frontline workers is now underway. As of 6 September 2026, a total of 2007 people had been vaccinated across six health zones in three provinces: Tshopo, Bas-U&eacute;l&eacute; and Ituri. </p><p>On 2 July, a clinical trial to find effective treatments against BVD began patient enrollment on 2 July. The trial, known as the PARTNERS trial, is now open in five different clinical management facilities in Ituri province, and has enrolled over 300 people who are confirmed cases. &nbsp;<br /></p>","Assessment":"<p>On 14 August 2026, WHO reassessed the risk of the outbreak of BVD, incorporating newly available information on the evolving situation. The risk for countries sharing land borders with the Democratic Republic of the Congo was separated from the risk for other countries in the African Region.</p><p>The risk in the Democratic Republic of the Congo was assessed as very high, the risk for countries sharing land borders with the Democratic Republic of the Congo was again assessed as high, and the risks for the rest of the African region and at the global level was again assessed as low.</p><p>For further information, please see <a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment-ebola-disease-caused-by-bundibugyo-virus--democratic-republic-of-the-congo-v4\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment-Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo v4</a><strong></strong></p>","Overview":"<p>Since the publication of the previous <a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON616\">Disease Outbreak News</a> on 28 August 2026, additional confirmed cases and deaths of Bundibugyo virus disease (BVD) have been reported only in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. </p><p>As of 7 September 2026, a cumulative total of 6778 confirmed cases has been reported: 6757 in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (including two cases diagnosed in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and subsequently treated in Germany), 20 cases in Uganda and one case in France. Overall, 3269 deaths have been reported, including two in Uganda. As of 7 September, at least 1611 patients have recovered, including 1590 in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, 18 in Uganda, two in Germany and one in France. </p><p>The sustained level of transmission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo continues to pose a risk of cross-border spread. Health screening and surveillance activities remain operational at airports, ports, and official land border crossings; however, travel through informal crossing routes persists and may facilitate virus exportation, importation, and subsequent transmission. In this context, strengthened cross-border coordination, together with ongoing surveillance and preparedness efforts, remains critical to limiting further regional spread and supporting an effective public health response.</p><p><strong>Figure 1. Distribution of cumulative confirmed cases of Bundibugyo virus disease in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, as of 7 September&nbsp;</strong></p><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/geographic-distribution-of-confirmed-cases037d941f-5da1-4704-8c1e-1ead73425f9f.jpg?sfvrsn=be8c79f9_4\" height=\"637\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"Geographic distribution of confirmed cases\" width=\"1004\" alt=\"Geographic distribution of confirmed cases\" sf-size=\"129991\" /></p><p><strong>Democratic Republic of the Congo </strong></p><p>Since the previous <a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON616\">Disease Outbreak News</a> was published on 28 August 2026, an additional 963 confirmed cases, including 481 confirmed deaths, have been reported in the Democratic Republic of the Congo. While part of this increase may be attributable to strengthened surveillance activities, enhanced laboratory testing, improved diagnostic capacity, and reconciliation of previously unreported data, the continued growth in both cases and deaths also reflects sustained community transmission and significant geographic expansion of the outbreak. As of 7 September, the Democratic Republic of the Congo has reported a total of 6757 confirmed cases, including 3267 deaths (CFR 48.3%). A total of 1590 patients have recovered to date.</p><p>Confirmed cases have been reported from 61 health zones (HZ) across six provinces, with 51 HZ from five provinces reporting at least one case in the last 21 days. Ituri remains the most affected province, with 28 of its 36 health zones reporting cases, followed by North Kivu (16/34), Tshopo (7/23), Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; (6/13), Bas-U&eacute;l&eacute; (3/11), and South Kivu (1/34). No new cases have been reported from South Kivu province since 29 May 2026. Kayna HZ in North Kivu province is the most recently affected area. As of 7 September, 71 new confirmed cases had been reported in the preceding 24 hours from 17 health zones located in Ituri, North Kivu, and Haut-U&eacute;l&eacute; provinces. </p><p>Ituri continues to be the epicentre of the outbreak, accounting for 5406 confirmed cases since the start of the outbreak, including 1114 new confirmed cases reported in the previous 21 days, as of 7 September. North Kivu is the second most affected province, with a cumulative number of 1066 confirmed cases, including 453 reported in the last 21 days, as of 7 September. One of the highest CFR (65.4%) observed in this outbreak has been reported from North Kivu province; and investigations are ongoing to better understand the factors contributing to this elevated mortality rate.</p><p>The number of individuals requiring follow-up as contact has also risen considerably with the expansion of the outbreak. As of 7 September, 85.3% of identified contacts were successfully monitored during the previous 24 hours with 21 359 contacts seen out of 24 719 requiring follow up. The large volume of contacts under surveillance highlights the extent of potential exposure within affected communities and the substantial demands placed on response operations.</p><p>The outbreak continues to unfold within a complex humanitarian setting characterized by insecurity, armed conflict, and widespread population displacement. More than 26 million people are experiencing acute food insecurity, while approximately one million internally displaced persons reside in Ituri Province alone. Ongoing insecurity and displacement limit access to healthcare and essential services, constrain the ability of response teams to reach affected areas, and impede surveillance, case investigation and contact tracing activities. Overcrowding, limited water, sanitation and hygiene services, and restricted access to healthcare in mining communities, informal settlements and sites for internally displaced persons further undermine early case detection, infection prevention and control measures, and the provision of timely care. These conditions also reduce the effectiveness of response interventions and outreach efforts. </p><p><strong>Figure 2: Number of confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, by date of notification, as of 7 September 2026</strong></p><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/number-of-confirmed-cases.jpg?sfvrsn=f68b6ab5_4\" height=\"617\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"Number of confirmed cases\" width=\"906\" alt=\"Number of confirmed cases\" sf-size=\"94940\" /></p><p><strong>Figure 3: Number of deaths among confirmed Bundibugyo virus disease cases in the Democratic Republic of the Congo by date of notification, as of 7 September 2026.&nbsp;</strong></p><p><img sf-image-responsive=\"true\" src=\"https://cdn.who.int/media/images/default-source/products/disease-outbreak-news/number-of-deaths.jpg?sfvrsn=c705223c_5\" height=\"636\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" title=\"Number of deaths\" width=\"934\" alt=\"Number of deaths\" sf-size=\"93163\" /></p>","DonId":"2026-DON617","Provider":"dynamicProvider372"},{"Id":"c835cb1d-25ad-4dd9-b065-ee09c054fe81","LastModified":"2026-08-28T17:12:36Z","PublicationDate":"2026-08-28T15:31:07Z","DateCreated":"2026-08-28T15:31:07Z","IncludeInSitemap":true,"SystemSourceKey":null,"UrlName":"2026-DON616","ItemDefaultUrl":"/2026-DON616","Response":"<p><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">For detailed information about the ongoing public health response actions by the Ministry of Health, WHO and partners please refer to the latest situation reports published by the WHO Regional Office for Africa:  </span><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" style=\"text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;white-space:inherit;\">Ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></p><div><p paraid=\"2108908504\" paraeid=\"{8b6d521b-ad2e-4ce4-a063-fe43d1a8742c}{44}\">Health authorities in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in collaboration with WHO and partners, are continuing to implement and coordinate extensive public health measures, including disease surveillance, laboratory testing, infection prevention and control, clinical care, community engagement, research, logistics and support for response interventions and essential health services, engaging donors and mobilizing additional resources to address critical funding gaps and sustain response operations across affected and at-risk areas. A substantial scale-up is ongoing across all response pillars to get ahead of the outbreak.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div>","FurtherInformation":"<div><p paraid=\"1812528342\" paraeid=\"{2b500c00-0d7b-4b25-81fa-fc8a6ea98695}{140}\"><strong>Regular Information products on the outbreak of Bundibugyo virus disease&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong></p></div><div><p paraid=\"1189967233\" paraeid=\"{2b500c00-0d7b-4b25-81fa-fc8a6ea98695}{152}\">Daily update: <a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/alert-and-response\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Epidemiological update on BVD outbreak in Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"224939789\" paraeid=\"{2b500c00-0d7b-4b25-81fa-fc8a6ea98695}{164}\">Published weekly: <a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Ongoing outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo | WHO | Regional Office for Africa</a> | WHO| Regional Office for Africa&nbsp;&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1653549980\" paraeid=\"{2b500c00-0d7b-4b25-81fa-fc8a6ea98695}{178}\">Published biweekly:<a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\"> Disease Outbreak News | All Hazards Public Health Events, Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>&nbsp;</p></div><div><p paraid=\"1519181916\" paraeid=\"{2b500c00-0d7b-4b25-81fa-fc8a6ea98695}{200}\"><strong>Additional information&nbsp;</strong></p></div><div><p paraid=\"835228782\" paraeid=\"{2b500c00-0d7b-4b25-81fa-fc8a6ea98695}{216}\">Current outbreak: declarations and status&nbsp;</p></div><ul paraid=\"1995642401\" paraeid=\"{2b500c00-0d7b-4b25-81fa-fc8a6ea98695}{222}\"><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/05-06-2026-africa-cdc-and-who-launch-joint-continental-ebola-response-plan\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Africa CDC and WHO launch joint continental Ebola response plan</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/publications/bundibugyo-ebola-virus-continental-preparedness-and-response-plan-june-november-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Bundibugyo Ebola virus | Continental preparedness and response plan: June-November 2026</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Epidemic of Ebola Disease caused by Bundibugyo virus in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda determined a public health emergency of international concern</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://administration.sante.gouv.cd/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Declaration-de-la-17e-Epidemie-de-la-maladie-a-virus-Ebola-dans-les-zones-de-sante-de-Rwampara-Mongwalu-et-Bunia-dans-la-province-dIturi.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">The Ministry of Public Health, Hygiene and Social Welfare, DRC, officially declares the 17th Ebola Disease outbreak</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/democratic-republic-congo-confirms-new-ebola-outbreak-who-scales-upsupport\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">WHO Democratic Republic of Congo confirms new Ebola outbreak</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/28-05-2026-message-by-the-who-director-general-to-the-people-of-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Message by the WHO Director-General to the people of the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/frontline-trust-day-julienne-anoko-whos-ebola-community-engagement-officer-drc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">At the frontline of trust: a day with Julienne Anoko WHO's Ebola Community Engagement Officer in DRC</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/democratic-republic-of-congo/news/decentralized-testing-speeds-ebola-response-democratic-republic-congo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Decentralized testing speeds up Ebola response in the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/uganda/news/uganda-ends-ebola-outbreak-following-completion-42-day-countdown\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" style=\"text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;white-space:inherit;\">Uganda ends Ebola outbreak following completion of 42-day countdown | WHO | Regional Office for Afri&hellip;</a><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">&nbsp;</span></li><li><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\"></span><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/24-08-2026-second-meeting-of-the-ihr-emergency-committee-on-the-epidemic-of-ebola-bundibugyo-virus-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-temporary-recommendations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\" style=\"text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;white-space:inherit;\">Second meeting of the IHR Emergency Committee on the epidemic of Ebola Bundibugyo virus disease in the Democratic Republic of the Congo &ndash; Temporary recommendations</a><span style=\"background-color:transparent;text-align:inherit;text-transform:inherit;word-spacing:normal;caret-color:auto;white-space:inherit;\">&nbsp;</span></li></ul><div><p paraid=\"2140528026\" paraeid=\"{6c4ffb26-0a96-469c-bd40-ff5d12237c6b}{163}\">WHO Rapid risk assessments&nbsp;</p></div><div><ul paraid=\"1918588806\" paraeid=\"{6c4ffb26-0a96-469c-bd40-ff5d12237c6b}{169}\"><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/publications/m/item/who-rapid-risk-assessment-ebola-disease-caused-by-bundibugyo-virus--democratic-republic-of-the-congo-v4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">WHO Rapid Risk Assessment-Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus, Democratic Republic of the Congo v4</a>&nbsp;</li></ul></div><div><p paraid=\"1916534432\" paraeid=\"{3965728f-7a29-4f6a-b1e7-c07ab4fba114}{47}\">Epidemiological updates and situation reports&nbsp;</p></div><ul paraid=\"1133912467\" paraeid=\"{3965728f-7a29-4f6a-b1e7-c07ab4fba114}{57}\"><li><a href=\"https://www.afro.who.int/health-topics/ebola-disease/outbreak-drc-26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Weekly External Situation Report. EBOLA BUNDIBUGYO VIRUS DISEASE OUTBREAK Democratic Republic of the Congo | Uganda.</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/ebola/situation-summary/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Ebola Outbreak: Current Situation | Ebola | CDC</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://insp.cd/category/sitrep/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Daily situation report, Ministry of Health, Democratic Republic of the Congo.</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://health.go.ug/press-statements/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Press Statements - Ministry of Health - Uganda</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://evd-daily.health.go.ug/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Ebola updates, Ministry of Health, Uganda</a>&nbsp;&nbsp;</li></ul><div><p paraid=\"1597093303\" paraeid=\"{3965728f-7a29-4f6a-b1e7-c07ab4fba114}{129}\">Published Disease Outbreak News (current outbreak)&nbsp;</p></div><ul paraid=\"335222661\" paraeid=\"{3965728f-7a29-4f6a-b1e7-c07ab4fba114}{141}\"><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON602\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda 16 May 2026</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON603\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo. 21 May 2026</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON605\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo. 29 May 2026</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON606\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda 8 June 2026</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON607\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda 13 June 2026</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON608\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Disease outbreak news. Ebola disease caused by Bundibugyo virus - Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda 19 June 2026</a>&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON612\" target